= 0.1 The Circle Closes <the-circle-closes>
Five there must be, and five there are.

#smallcaps[\~]

A queen walks a wooded path.

A seer watches, yearning for and dreading what she must witness.

A hunter stalks a figure on a bridge.

A king presses his signet ring into a molten wax seal.

A man-at-arms stands at the base of a tower.

#smallcaps[\~]

The queen strides with purpose, lest her path stray.

The seer sees the dance of the closing circle, and she knows whose step is off.

The hunter draws up to the monument of a giant’s blade, plunged into the mortar of the bridge and alight with pale fire.

The king settles back into his throne, his eyes following his sealed orders from footman to mounted knight.

The man-at-arms peers up at a balcony on the tower, as fallen foes smoke in piles around him.

\~

Mere, the Queen-errant, comes upon a parting of the ways. It is not only the woods that part, but also the dappled sunlight, and the illusion of place itself. Mere steps through, onto fine stonemasonry.

Huddled under a blanket in her snowed-in cave, Kella Aspenwood notes the soundless footfall of a queen’s boot on stone. She is in Mere’s footsteps and Lenn’s absence. She feels Anshall’s nerves and Persaval’s flames . They are as one with her, an Extension of her being, as they must be. Her Circle, drawing across the realm toward a singular purpose.

At the foot of an angel, Lost Lenn places a bold hand on an ancient weapon. A question hangs in the air, unspoken, ringing. “What purpose brings you to this place?”

Anshall, King on the Far Mountain, speaks nothing to his advisors and attendants as he watches one of his Knight-Heralds trot through his palace courtyards. All that was articulate in him has been poured into the orders the knight carries. Instead, he waits. His part is easiest to play, and his dance the simplest in steps. Five there must be, and they must be funded.

In the shadow of the tower’s balcony, Persaval of Ea’river raises a sword whose white-lacquered blade has never been honed to an edge. The wind dies. A red hot flame rises with the sword, engulfing Persaval.

\~

Mere has come to render aid that was owed, but for a moment she hesitates. She wants to hear, perhaps for the last time, the Interrogator play his game of questions.

Kella, watching, steals a breath for herself—for the woman shivering in the cold of the realm into which she was born.

Lenn greets the angel’s question of purpose with a smile on his lips. “How does one come to the land beyond your watch?” He opens his hands to the emptiness beyond the balustrade of the bridge. To retain some sanity, he imagines it a vast black space.

The angel decides to play Lenn’s game. It returns, “To what end does this path bring you?”

Anshall steeples his fingers beneath his crown and prays.

The balcony burns. Its bannisters crack and fall to the courtyard below. The very stones groan underfoot. Amidst the flames walk their master, Persaval with his burning sword, closing on the arch that leads into the chambers of the tower’s master. The silhouette of a man appears in the doorway. The balcony resolves into dew.

\~

Mere takes another gentle, silent footstep on the Bridge. Behind her, the parting of the ways closes. She will not be returning the way she came. There is little time, but the questions hold her captive.

Kella shivers.

Lenn asks, “To whose end does it lead?”

“Who can force a soul beyond my vigil without consent?” the angel probes.

With that question, it is confirmed. The steps were off, and now Kella knows it was not a failure of Lenn’s self-control, but a black spot on years of careful planning. They cannot succeed. The circle’s trap will close on nothing but air.

Anshall’s lips move soundlessly.

“Hello Thrast,” says Persaval, his presence obscured by his flames.

Perhaps during times of peace Thrast, He of Titles and Gifts Unutterable, still keeps chambers recognizable as human residence. But his tower is presently afflicted with a burning nuisance, and Thrast will not afford Persaval of Ea’river the respite of cover. His inner chamber is now bare, a circular high-ceilinged room of plain gray stone and, as of moments ago, no doors in or out. Within, Persaval circles the apparition of Thrast’s body. This is a formality.

The grand wizard Thrast, tyrant of Ea’river, scourge of civilization, lets out a sigh. He holds out a placating hand towards where Persaval might be in the smoke and flames, his whole body performing a studied bleeding of its tension, from shoulders to knees. “What is your hope for this gambit?”

\~

Mere feels the pressure of the Bridge now. There is the immaculate and lifeless path leading to an angel who guards a forbidden realm, and apart from that there is only looming and indifferent absence. She has not come to help Lenn kill a divine being, but to see to it that his steps align with the dance, and to assure him he is not forgotten. But the dance is meaningless, their hopes a jest that must have already been foreseen and laughed away. The angel will certainly refuse to allow Thrast beyond. “Lenn,” she says.

It is all Kella can do not to retreat, to draw the lines of her self back across leagues and Wayward Paths to be secure in her own fragile, bone-cold body. But there is a line, however tenuous: a question hanging in the air. If she falters, all is truly lost.

Lost Lenn turns just enough to meet Mere’s eyes, but his hand never leaves the cool, burning, ancient sword. If he is shaken by the angel’s last question, it shows not on his face. Instead, he offers her assurance in the only way he can. “Can you follow through despite?”

The others of the Circle are masters of their craft. Surely Anshall’s burden is light in comparison, but still it presses him to his knees. He feels a turning come, through some tenuous connection, perhaps a touch from Aspenwood. Hope? Despair. No matter. If they fail, then Thrast will visit him before very long at all, and then his best hope will be for Thrast to reduce the people of his kingdom to ash. Follow through, he prays, else the world will strangle.

The light from Persaval’s flames begins to reflect another place: a field of green and golden grasses waving in gentle breeze. Somewhere just out of sight stands a humble cabin, from which wafts the smell of fresh bread.

Persaval’s lips peel back from his teeth.

Thrast presses on. “You are not enough of a fool to think you can end me here, alone.” He steps up to one blazing wall and watches the idyllic vision in the flames. “But you have much at stake, which you believe I threaten.”

The heat of these flames will not touch Persaval. The fires are his. But the smell of woodsmoke billowing acrid over the waft of fresh bread catches him, and the hint of cooking human flesh cuts through bone. “Threaten?” Persaval growls. “Ask Qastra’s Shining Legion if you are a threat.”

Thrast puts a pair of fingers to his temple, as if to nurse a headache. As if the body in this sweltering chamber is anything more than a puppet dangling on the wizard’s strings. There is a single piece of jewelry on that body: a glittering amber star. Sometimes it is an earring, or adorning a fine band, or hanging from a chain around his neck. But it never remains in one place for long.

Thrast says, “You #underline[know];.” He manufactures a huff. “Persaval, I showed you my vision. Can you prevent the Ruin that is coming? Because I #underline[can];.”

\~

Mere has gone.

Kella strains to maintain the line drawn between the Wayward Queen and Persaval’s rage. It frays.

Lenn, leaning with his palm against the pale, burning sword of an angel, asks, “Is there a soul who can coax Thrast beyond?”

The angel replies, “Is there a soul to whom Thrast belongs?”

Lenn tugs on the still-gleaming cord of the Circle’s power, tied to him as to the others. He warns Kella, across two realms, to brace herself. “Can a soul be made to belong with Thrast?”

The angel’s head turns down from its vigil, only briefly. “Ought there to be a soul bound up so?”

And Lenn, sighing and turning away from the Bridge to face another realm, distant and inert, answers, “There ought.”

Anshall murmurs the sealing word of his prayer.

“Your prudence holds you hostage,” Thrast accuses. “At least attempt to strike me down. A shame, but I should like to see how exquisite your pyre will be.”

Persaval’s voice breaks. “Do you think this is righteousness? Souls reclaimed to your senseless purpose? Nations bent to your one twisted will? How can you stand there so sure that what you do is right?”

“Right?” The sound of laughter fills the room like a thunderclap. A wizard’s laugh, but Thrast’s hand tips, for his body’s mouth does not move at all. “Boy, I do what is necessary.”

Behind Thrast’s puppeted body, a path opens that was not there before.

Persaval says, “If you are not stopped you will bring ruin to every nation in this realm.”

Before Thrast can answer, a sword of gold and gray severs the arm from his body. The arm immediately fades into dust, but the armband that houses an amber star clatters, however briefly, to the floor. Then Mere, Arch-herald, delivers a message from the Circle, communicating the spirit of a missive which had been sent for this very tower signed and sealed by a king. “Thrast, begone.”

The bonds of the Circle shatter.

\~

When the haze of the moment passes, and as the flames die, there stands in a scorched tower chamber a man-at-arms, a wayward queen, and David Huxley Adamson holding a dripping toilet brush.

= Part One: Association <part-one-association>
= 1.1 Instant Metadimensional Transit <instant-metadimensional-transit>
== David, Lila <david-lila>
The young man before Mere and Persaval is out of place. Around them is a smoldering room. That this room mounts a tower is impossible to tell at the moment. Instead it is only a bare, circular cell with no doors or windows, and it is wreathed in firelight. The tool in the young man\'s hand drips with water, which causes a pile of embers at his feet to steam. Persaval is in the white coat of his Arbiter\'s office, trimmed and untouched by the flames. Mere wears traveling leathers inlaid with the various badges of the Arch-messenger. David wears a brown t-shirt that says “Off” and dusty sweatpants.

David’s confused for starters. ~He was, to no one’s surprise, not prepared for instant metadimensional transit. He’s seen this sort of thing before, in books and movies that captured his imagination. But it’s not his imagination that’s been captured. It is his soul. He feels it, as smoky air passes over his tongue and fills his lungs. There’s a knot tied in his brain, or a loop slipped around his intestines, some tether hooked into the very center of him. But what is on the other end? To David\'s eyes, a man with a blazing torch and a woman with a golden sword stand at a threatening closeness, looking as surprised as he is. As far as he takes in his surroundings, it is to know that he is trapped. There is a pause.

It is the man with the torch who reacts first. “Putting on a different face will not deter us!” He hefts the torch in both hands and takes it like a bat to David’s head. David, stunned, doesn’t yet flinch. The rod at the center of the flames bounces off his head with a dull thud. David feels slightly warmer, but otherwise unaffected.

Seeing that the blow has no effect, Mere drives her sword for David’s heart. An inch from his chest the tip of the blade skirls aside with a shriek and slips harmlessly between David’s arm and chest. It is not just the tip of the sword that shrieks, but David as well. He shrinks away belatedly.

The man and woman pause. They exchange looks. They turn back to David, expecting #underline[something];. Whatever they expect, it doesn’t come.

David rocks back onto his butt.

The expectation on their faces melts into horror, held for a long quiet moment. The torch goes out, leaving lingering fire around the edges of the room to light the space.

The man with the torch sinks into a squat. He’s no longer young, but he is still poised like a dancer on the balls of his feet, ready to spring not as an intention but out of habit. David has seen fighters in the ring, and actors playing parts, but he’s never sat a few feet from an armed man who is ready to do harm. A day\'s worth of peppered stubble covers the man’s face. To the woman the man says, “Mere?”

The woman lets her sword-arm drop. She steps closer to David, cautious if not timid. “You were not meant to take his place,” she says. “I am sorry for your loss.”

“My loss.” David blinks.

The man says, “We intended to dispel a great evil from our land. I believe he is gone. But here you are in his place… and with his qualities.”

David says, “The clubbing and stabbing.”

“Without effect,” Mere agrees.

“Scaring me half to death is an effect.”

Persaval sighs. “The curse did not work as intended. Everything is veiled.”

The woman keeps her eyes on David. “Should I bring Lenn to question him?”

“Is it worth the cost?” the man responds.

Mere stops, and shakes her head, and mutters, “Is anything?”

David\'s eyes fall on a pile of embers near his shoes. A shudder runs down his back.

“That was his body once.” The man stands and gestures at the embers. “I pray it takes him some time to find another.”

And, mocking the pretense of finding his bearings, David\'s gut drops. “If I\'m in his place,” he asks, “is he in mine?”

Mere is quick to say, “He was meant to be banished somewhere barren and empty, not a realm with people to subdue.”

“How did you fail?” David rises. “Who taught you the spell?”

Persaval says, “It was the work of years, the product of a Circle of masters.”

“A shot in the dark.” David finds himself advancing on the pair. “You took a risk, and the price was setting a monster loose on my world.” He is not afraid of them. They have proven and admitted they can’t hurt him. But there is a glint of anxiety in #underline[their] eyes, as he steps closer.

“My sister is coming over today. What will she find when she steps through my door?”

The flames in the room burn lower.

Mere says, “With mercy, a swift death.”

\~

The car that pulls into the Adamson driveway is a little plastic-hulled Saturn from the turn of the century. The driveway the car pulls into is short. Car and driveway end up in a garage that\'s been annexed with a shed so that cars can actually fit inside the garage proper. As it pulls in, the car is thrumming with the music that fills its cabin, a hurlant rhapsody of guitar riffs and dark crashes. The car parks. The music cuts. Its occupant emerges.

Lila Adamson is the grace to David\'s gangle, her back straight, her lines long, and her hair tucked just to the side. Beneath her open plaid button-up is a t-shirt that reads, “On.”

She is halfway out of the car when she hears the noise. It sounds like someone is ransacking her parents’ kitchen.

Out comes Lila’s phone, dialing David. We know why he doesn’t answer. “You’ve reached David. Leave your message when the lady says.”

Beep.

“Call me back.”

She doesn’t call her parents. She doesn’t call the police. This neglect is instinctual.

Dad\'s golf clubs are right there. Most everything else has been stuffed into the annex to make room for using the garage as a garage, but Dad goes golfing at least twice a month. She grabs the club with the biggest head. This she wields openly, and as a distraction from the little pruning shears she tucks into her belt, hidden by the hem of her shirt.

Lila steps through the garage door and past the mud room. The noise grows both louder and harder to parse. The crashes of glass jars and thunks of boxes are accompanied by a sound like starch-thickened water circling a drain and a stretching like a rubber glove over a hand that is too small for it.

In the kitchen, Lila finds a storm of pantry odds and refrigerated ends whorling around an amber shape in the eye of the storm. The faucet is running in the sink, but the water is falling into the storm. A carton of eggs skitters out of the open fridge. Three eggs come loose and crack open, the contents graying and burning and #underline[stretching] into the surface of a face in the same way it seems cookie dough has for the shoulders and a fruit roll-up across the thigh.

In nature flesh takes its time, and its privacy, to shape itself. A living being spends weeks and months of hidden work knitting gathered resources into material according to the available prescribed methods, and after that the being toils through a lifetime of quiet, steady upkeep best maintained by a balanced schedule of material intake. Thrast does not have the patience to respect privacy or nature\'s course. Divorced from true reliance on a body and empowered by the heights of skill in magic, he claims anything and everything in the Adamson kitchen as his own, and puts it to use.

Lila yelps.

A pitch black pupil forms in egg white. An apple peels itself and approximates a mouth. Behind literal apple-red lips, a slurry of material gurgles into the shape of a wind pipe.

Lila does not wait for the nightmare in front of her to speak. The golf club drops from her hands and she snatches up a fire extinguisher. She pulls the pin. She aims and fires. A pale frosty cloud wraps around the shape of a man.

“To what end this violence?” The thing’s voice is tinny, and sounds like it is coming from the sink.

Lila has closed in, pruning shears in hand. She stabs at the amber stone glinting in the afternoon light. Her stab clinks aside.

“Is this the first thought in your head? To destroy what you do not understand?”

Dust swirls and a force presses Lila back. It holds her at arms’ length. A cloud of powdery white with an apple mouth and misaligned eyes regards her.

“You should not fear me, woman.”

“You are trespassing in my parents’ house.” Lila steps back, to break the feeling of pressure against her shoulders.

“Trespassing? I did not come here by my own will.” The humanoid shape is still locked in a gesture of contemplation. There is no anxiety in its bearing.

Lila’s shoulders are stiff, and her pupils are wide. She says, “You’re breaking our kitchen.”

The shape moves from gesture to gesture like a puppet, even as its “flesh” fills out. “I have need for it. A body is unnecessary, but serves its purposes.”

“Why #underline[our] house?”

“You would need to ask the ones who banished me.”

Lila pounces, “And why were you banished, thing?”

The not-man hesitates only a moment before answering Lila’s question. “It is the nature of the yolked to buck against the man who drives the plow.”

“We’re not livestock.”

“And I do not preside over beasts.” The door of the fridge tears loose and there is a whining as the steel is warped into the shape of a breastplate around the “chest” of the cloud-man. The cloud finishes burning to an ashy tone, with a consistency somewhere between skin and balloon. ~“My name is Thrast. Tell me about the world to which I have been exiled.”

“You can\'t just--” Lila\'s tongue freezes in place behind her teeth.

“Do you know the first thing I discovered when I arrived, Lila Adamson?” Thrast’s “body” shows no outward sign of effort, but a force locks every joint in Lila’s body. It holds her breath until she thinks her lungs may burst, then releases her diaphragm to force the bad air out. As he allows a new breath into her lungs, Thrast says, “Your world has no magic of its own. Most fortuitously, Domination and Separation are not present to shield the sovereign will of even the most sophisticated creature.”

Something worms its way into her mind and finds a comfortable place to sit.

#emph[So tell me, you spectacularly helpless thing, how this world is doomed.]

\~

David wonders if he has felt this sort of terror before. It is like watching a car stuck on the tracks with a train barreling on. He feels the need to shout for the person in the car.

“Fix it.” He growls, leaning in towards the man and woman.

“We cannot snap our fingers and bring him back,” Persaval says.

“And you wouldn\'t if you could.”

They open their mouths to defend themselves.

“No, no, I get it. He’s The End for wherever he is, right? And right now he’s in my house, waiting to eat my sister and flay my parents.”

Mere says, “It will take some time, but we can seek a solution.”

“Does my world even have time left?” David bounces on the balls of his feet. “Maybe I can console myself that his first taste of Earth was probably the toilet.” He briefly and furiously scratches one of his ears. “Augh, no, gotta stop thinking about #underline[there];. Tell me about #underline[here];. Where am I?”

Mere begins to speak, but Persaval lifts a hand to try to stop her. They exchange looks. He shakes his head. She hardens her eyes. She says, “Ea’river. It was once a Free State. Until moments ago, it was a district under the Dictates of Thrast. This Realm is widely called Veneth, and it is governed by Association.”

David says, “I understood the first half of that. What\'s the name of the language we\'re speaking?”

Persaval, sighing, says, “Low Speech, the language of merchants, craftsmen, and the common man.”

“With a handful of King\'s Words,” Mere adds.

“Then I guess with whatever else, I\'ve got your guy\'s language. Names?”

Mere repeats hers, but Persaval stands silent.

After a long enough pause, David grunts. “Rude.”

“This is a realm governed by Association,” Persaval repeats. “To give my name is to present an open gate to my Domination.”

“Then why did Mere give me hers?”

“If,” Mere poses, “you had access to Domination--which I suspect you don\'t--and if you knew how to use it--which I know you don\'t--then you could try to Command me, but even Thrast in his arrogance would not take the risk. Else I would have long since crafted him a maze of passage with no means of escape.”

“Yeah,” David says. “There are worse things you can do to a person than stab them.” To Persaval, “Maybe I\'ll call you Galahad.”

“You may call me by my title: Arbiter.”

“Nah, I\'ve decided. You\'re Mustang.” David checks the walls of the room again. “How do you leave a room with no doors?”

Mere says, “You make one.”

David pauses for a beat. “Well?”

“Where will you go in this world you know nothing about?”

“Somewhere that’s not a room with no doors. Maybe even somewhere not on fire.”

Mustang says, “You will accomplish as little out there as you will in this chamber.”

David gestures between the two. “So I say, ‘I want to leave’ and you say, ‘No.’ Is it trust you’re missing or are you still trying to work out how to take advantage of me?”

“I think I preferred when you were stunned,” Mustang mutters.

“Stunned silence about my #underline[planet] that you’ve murdered? Excuse me if I find it more palatable to chatter than brood. Because clearly brooding isn’t getting anything done. In this empty room. With no doors or windows.”

There is, as he finishes speaking, a door looming behind Mere. It must stand almost ten feet from the floor to the ceiling, and it is formed entirely from a dark and glossy wood. Where the handle should be, there is a large gilded keyhole.

“Whatever else you may have of Thrast’s,” Mere says, “you lack his patience. This is a relief.”

Persaval tuts. “Thrast is only patient when it suits his purpose.”

David feels a tug, in his brain or his gut. “This Thrast you swapped me with. Is he a man or a beast?”

Mere answers, “A meaningless distinction.”

\~

If Thrast thought the young woman he had dominated would be powerless against his magic, he is being corrected now. Even when her breath only comes at his behest, and with his firm grip on her mind, Lila Adamson does not capitulate. There is terror in her that shakes her body beyond Thrast\'s ability to keep her still, and there is a certitude of purpose no single wave of extraplanar magic can overcome.

Thrast chides himself for having been too hasty and for trusting impressions without testing them thrice. No matter that this young woman lacks magic to shield her from his Domination, she has the innate magic of #underline[being] and #underline[selfhood];, assets she has honed to a knife-edge. If Thrast wants the knowledge of Lila Adamson, he must convince her to give over her knowledge by conventional means, or he must take her apart.

The words her body cannot put into the air reach Thrast anyway. #underline[You can\'t come and go as you please. You can\'t make demands and see them answered. Not here, and not with me. Let me go, and ask me nicely, and we\'ll see what I say];#emph[.]

Thrast finishes forming the prime human instrument. Easier than creating strange resonances in appliances. “I feel your mind quivering,” he says with a fully formed set of human speech organs. “If I release you, you will reach out with the device in your back pocket and contact enforcers to help subdue me. Or your parents. You are not sure which.”

Motionless, Lila’s surface thoughts skitter across his fingers of Extension: her family and friends in flashes too symbolic and brief for Thrast to parse; her half-conscious mind struggling frantically to put Thrast into a category it can process, apart from #underline[person] and #underline[terrible];.

“I do not wish to escalate a conflict without reason,” Thrast continues. “But in the minutes since my arrival I have already seen several indications that this world is in as much peril as my own was, before the latter doomed itself and ejected me.”

“What indications?” he allows her to ask.

“There is a tang in the air,” Thrast answers, “and the first person I meet immediately tries to destroy me before breathing a word to me.”

#underline[A tang in the air? Really?]

He continues to amass himself an approximation of human form with the matter available to him. There is a nudge from the part of his being that was once an animal and would be again, to take the shortcut and use the extant human flesh in the room to remake himself. It is not Lila\'s will that prevents him here, but his own. He has not yet confirmed that there is a need for such lengths.

“What is wrong with this world?” he asks again.

#underline[I have a lot of answers to that question, but none of them are for someone who is measuring out my breaths.]

“Surely there are more pliant people in the world than yourself.” Thrast glances out the kitchen window. “Perhaps I should try with them.”

There is a subtle release in Lila’s psyche, and he lets her speak and breathe freely. “What\'s wrong is people like you,” Lila says, “who measure themselves with a meritometer at off-the-charts levels, and believe that this entitles them to whatever they think best without regard to the people who stand in their way. Violent, arrogant, manipulative patches of fire extinguisher haze with God complexes.”

Thrast leans his ash-crusted body towards the woman. “And what have these people done to your world that needs fixing?”

“Too much to list.”

“But if you had to pick somewhere to start?”

A scornful wrinkle to the nose. “Are you offering to fix the planet?”

“I\'m not offering.” Thrast turns away from Lila\'s incredulity. He peers out the window at a squirrel that is running up a tree, and he remembers the vision that drives him. He shares this memory with the woman who does not believe his sincerity.

Crusts of earth drifting in a soundless void.

Husks of bygone civilization huddled shapeless in endless shattered emptiness.

“I have seen the future, and I will not suffer its coming.”

\~

Mere slides her sword into the new door’s keyhole. There is a soft hiss, like the seal breaking on a soda bottle.

Persaval says, “We should discuss this.”

“What is there to discuss?” Mere challenges. Her sword is still in the door like an over-long key, and the gentle hiss continues around the edges of the door. “I have duties to attend which require me not to linger in the eyrie of a banished wizard’s tower.”

“And the boy?”

“Who is, yes, standing right here.” David edges towards the door. They might not be able to hurt him, but you can cage someone without stabbing them.

Mere turns her face away from the men. “He can do no more harm than Thrast.”

Persaval’s gesture of capitulation is to slide the white-lacquered torch into a scabbard. It occurs to David that it must be some kind of sword, but without an edge it seems more like a club.

Mere pulls the door open. The space beyond it is black. David’s first thought is it must be night outside the room, but as he approaches the doorway he sees that instead of the night sky there is a huge stone bridge in an endless, teetering void. A~short set of stairs connects their door to the bridge. Across the bridge is another set of stairs up to another door, this one glimmering and translucent.

“No,” Persaval says. “I will not go this way.”

“Fine.” Mere shrugs. “Burn your way out like you burned your way in.”

David’s attention is drawn to the hair on Persaval’s knuckles, which stand on end to match those on the back of David’s neck. He says, “What’s wrong with the Bridge, Mustang?”

Persaval regards him with wide eyes. “First among many, that this is your first time seeing it and you know exactly what it is.”

David tries to shrug it off by saying, “It’s a bridge.” What comes out instead is, “It is an aimless causeway.”

“It’s a straight crossing,” Mere assures Persaval. His name is on the tip of her tongue when she says this, but she holds it back. “No turns, no wending. As safe a passage as we can make.”

“Destination?”

“Not until we cross.”

Persaval grumbles, “I’m coming.”

David follows Mere across the threshold. His head becomes immediately lighter, but he manages his next question anyway. “We don\'t get to know the destination because knowing might jeopardize the crossing?”

The flavor of Mere\'s silence is a yes.

There is no hand-rail on the steps leading down to the bridge, leaving you just a clumsy step away from an eternal fall into oblivion. David moves carefully: step, shuffle, step, shuffle, step; he is clear onto broad, guarded stone. When David turns his head and looks up the bridge (or down it, there’s no way to tell), his eyes water and he locks his gaze on Mere’s back.

Behind him, Persaval is dead-quiet. The man’s steps, breath, and clothing make no sound. David’s only way of knowing that the man has followed is Persaval’s hand resting on David’s shoulder.

Each step across the bridge, David tries to focus on Mere. She is one stride ahead of him, moving with the confidence of a captain walking the deck of her ship during a battle or a storm. There is tension there, but it has worn into a kind of comfort. Here on this bridge, which draws the mind out to lengths beyond sight and whose very existence sets David on edge, Mere is home. But the weight of the bridge’s path into motionless dark turns David\'s head to the side.

Persaval\'s hand on David\'s shoulder tightens. It feels like it\'s Persaval\'s fingers that give. Is this what stone feels when someone pushes on it? The man also pushes David forward, towards the shimmering door. It is only a few more steps, but David squeezes his eyes shut and lets Persaval guide him teetering up and through.

As they cross it feels like the thing David is tethered to yanks on the binding. His eyes snap open and he straightens, stumbling out of Persaval\'s reach and into a field of long, cool grass. The air is sweet, not smoke-filled like the room or--dear God there was no air in that place how did he not notice there was no air--

“The Bridge is a realm of Extension,” Mere says. “A many-crossed path. It is the easiest realm to reach from ours and so I use it to travel quickly across Veneth or to other realms that require my attention.”

“Many realms and many places throughout Veneth connect to the Bridge. Without discipline, you can find yourself leaving by a different path than you intended,” adds Persaval. “Or if your will is weak, you may be drawn to seek what is on the far side of the Bridge.” He fixes David with a pointed look.

“There is an end?” David hears a peal of thunder in the distance. They have come out into a large but thank God not endless field of breeze-blown grasses. With the sun to his left, he can see a cumulonimbus convention kicking off near the horizon.

“Of sorts,” Mere says. “But one must never make that crossing.” She says this while making eye contact with Persaval. They are agreed on withholding that information, apparently.

“So, Mustang, you dislike the Bridge for the same reason you won\'t tell me your real name?”

“I lost what was most precious to me on that Bridge.”

“Ah, okay, tragic backstory. Gotcha.”

“Are you mocking me?”

“No, I\'m…” David stands. He is parsing information as quickly as he can, paring things down into digestible pieces, because his whole body feels strange and #underline[this is not his place] and where he had been is now subject to some sort of death tyrant. David flexes his fingers. Don’t think about what could have happened where you’re not. Not until you can get back. “Fix-it time, kiddos.”

“There is no simple reversal for this,” Persaval says, visibly pushing down his frustration. “If there is a way to switch you back, it will take time, coordination, and effort. None of which we have much of.”

“You mustered enough for the first try.” There is a strong, bitter breeze blowing in the field they have come to. If this place has any significance to Mere or Persaval, David cannot see it on their faces. ~A shiver runs through him as the breeze raises goosebumps across his arms. “And I\'m discovering that I\'m very good at nagging when I think it\'s important.

“My name is David Adamson,” he tells Mere and Mustang. “I’m from Michigan on the planet Earth. As far as I know, Earth isn’t governed by a high concept, but the country I’m from is definitely governed by Profit. We don’t get to boot our tyrants across dimensions, but I suppose ours are easier to kill, as long as you can find them.

“There are eight billion people on Earth. I don’t know if that’s a number you have in Low Speak or if that’s just a nonsense word I just said. I can break it down for you if you like.”

David pauses a beat.

“A thousand thousands is a million. A thousand millions is a billion. We have eight billion people living on our planet. If your brain whimpered a little trying to comprehend that number I’m with you. Fun fact: we’re not wired to think of numbers of things at that scale. And every impression you’ve given me is that your helpful friend you switched me with is going to have his way with the lot of them.

“Should I start in on the various philosophical schools of thought that would establish your mistake as evil on a scale that, as I mentioned, defies comprehension? Or should I stick with personal anecdotes?

“You see my sister, Lila Adamson--that’s her name, Lila. Li’s the smartest person I know. Graduated with two degrees at once, with honors. She wants to study the stars--the actual physical attributes of them, not the lines they trace in our sky--and she wants to write poems about them--the lines they trace, not their physical attributes. But she knows more than just stars and poetry. She knows offhand the measurements of the amount of gases our eight billion people put out every year, and the mechanics behind how that is going to kill every single one of us by the end of this century, and the personal contact information of eight of our nation’s leaders who are actively ignoring the damage we’re doing to our very existence.

“She can do nothing about it. There are eight billion people in the world, and she is just one of them. Any one person’s actions are a drop in the bucket of the world stage, and still she strives to #emph[do right];.

“Today she’s going to come home to visit a brother who loves her, and instead she’s going to find some unspeakable horror you shipped off to Earth by accident, and he is going to kill her.”

Some incendiary device turns over behind Persaval’s eyes. His jaws clench and then release. He takes a step towards David. “You cannot guilt me into taking action, David Adamson. All you accomplish with this tirade is to distract me from thinking through our options. I beg pardon for your spectacularly doomed home, but if you can bear in silence long enough for me to #emph[think..];.”

“Tell me about magic,” David says. “How does it work? Apparently different worlds can be governed by… things… but what does that mean?”

Mere answers over whatever Persaval would have said, “There are five Paradigms by which a person interacts with the world. Reality flows according to those Paradigms.”

“Yeah, okay,” David says. “And these paradigms are?”

“Association, Extension, Domination, Edification, and Separation.” Mere takes a full breath of air, and seems to taste it.

Persaval sighs. “If you did not know their names, or how many there were, or that reality is thus structured, you would pose less danger to us.” He says it without breaking eye contact with Mere. “If you possess the same ability as Thrast, then you are Aligned with all five Paradigms.”

“How many do you have access to, Mustang?”

He glares for a beat. “Two.”

“I see our disagreement persists.” Mere says to Persaval. “I understand you are hesitant to risk giving an inch to Thrast, but first, he is gone, and second, your reticence antagonizes someone you have made clear you cannot afford to have as an enemy.”

“Will you teach him to scry, then? Perhaps you will guide him through creating a doorway?”

“Clearly if I stop talking there\'s plenty you can do to keep stalling,” David cuts in. “If you can travel worlds, Mere, then take me to Earth.”

She looks him dead in the eye and says, “No.”

“What!”

“Until I know more about what has happened, I will not consider meandering unknown realms in search of your home.” Mere turns her shoulders. “I must attend the others. Kella will reconnect us soon.”

Persaval bites his tongue.

Mere finishes turning, and then she is gone, stepping into another space entirely.

“That was sudden,” David says.

\~

Thrast first instructs, and then requests Lila to lead him outside. She obeys with stiff movements, not like a marionette but like a prisoner bound. Thrast is certain he has dominated stronger wills than hers. Entire nation-states have bowed before him, and others still have paid a high price for eternity. But this young woman is bold and unwavering in the face of the difference to their strength. And she is cooperating just enough for now.

Lila opens a door which leads out into a very lightly paved path amidst a moss-patched lawn. There is, just west of the lawn, a larger path constructed of some amalgam. Trees dot the yard, living records of the past. The air carries traces of smoke and acrid gases. There is a river within a mile. The door closes behind them.

“This is a strange realm,” Thrast says to Lila. “My first impression is that nothing here is truly real. Each thing sits empty of the essential magic of existence. I would guess, had I not already confirmed the truth to be opposite, that nothing in your world would respond to my magic. To the contrary, it is all ready, as if waiting, to receive the brush strokes of my experience.” As he speaks, he Associates the trees with each other, with the creatures that make homes of them, and the History of this homestead which he does not yet know.

Lila says, “We are in the United States of America. Specifically Michigan. It gets cold and snowy in the winter and hot and sweaty in the summer. Many of the people who live on this side of the state are rich and live down south in the sun during the winter. But that’s the elders for the most part. They made a living in their day.

“People are resistant to change. They want to keep what they already have at the expense of the people who have been trying to scrape by from the womb. Merchants pull strings in the government to let them consolidate more wealth and overturn those that encourage responsibility to the environment and to each other. The richest ones have decided it\'s better not to try to help and instead are funding private efforts to go to space.

“The U.S. in particular believes we are some sort of big brother to the rest of the world, and that it\'s our job to police other countries and make everyone look like us.

“Treaties are deteriorating. The U.S. is dividing itself over issues we thought we resolved in the last world war. Every month or so a different angry white man guns down people in a public space. Stable governments are being overthrown for reasons good and bad, and the regions are not finding equilibrium. Our icecaps are melting.”

“Everywhere,” Thrast summarizes, “people use the excuse of their limited perspective to nudge us all a little closer to oblivion.”

“And you want to prevent that end.” Lila is still stiff, though Thrast has released her. “How?”

Thrast Extends his being into the trees, down into their roots, and by Association into their History. He exerts Domination over them. The trunks of the trees creak. He Edifies them.

Two of the nearest trees groan as they fill out, rising a few inches higher and their branches reaching out and tangling together, until they become one tree with two trunks, bridged by their branches. Thrast withdraws from the trees.

“This world is wet clay, Lila Adamson,” he says. “I will shape it.”

= 1.2 A Soul is Thrown Into the Kiln <a-soul-is-thrown-into-the-kiln>
== Lila <lila>
A voice in some hidden corner whispers, “#underline[You’re in shock];.” For what feels like forever but has to be less than five minutes, everything is hazy. She’s afraid to risk stray thoughts when they could bring his full attention on her again. The few thoughts that crystallize are gems formed under the heat and pressure of his presence.

Thrast thumbs through her mind; he leaves an imprint.

Thrast calls it magic; a mystery he thinks he’s solved.

He’s careless; he names Domination and Separation in the same breath.

Lila is now a thing-to-do-with-Thrast; Thrast changes the trees and they become Thrast-things too.

They aren’t his. SHE’S NOT HIS.

His touch lingers.

Thrast leaves Lila, promising to return after he gets his bearings in this countryside. Leaving is another mistake. While he is gone, Lila is free to move and to think.

With every moment Thrast spends on Earth, he sees, and smells, and hears, and touches the world. Every interaction, every stimulus, passes through him, and both he and it are changed. This doesn’t make him special. It’s how people synthesize the experience of reality. But no one on Earth is from Somewhere Else. Thrast doesn’t know what he did. He thinks he’s left her a crumb in passing, but he’s actually left the faucet running. Magic unfurls itself to Lila like the blossoming fire of a wound to the chest, and it is the irreducible assignation of meaning.

This is Association.

A stab of adrenaline clears her thoughts. She snaps back through the door into her parents’ house. It slams behind her.

“David!”

The house is silent.

“David, where are you?”

If something like Thrast came into the house while David was there, what might Thrast have done to David? She follows a trail of water soaked into the rugs and carpet between the kitchen and the upstairs bathroom. No David. There’s a basket of cleaning supplies scattered on the floor. The toilet paper is gone. The medicine cabinet is empty. A couple sections of the hardwood flooring are missing. There’s no blood, or hair, or skin.

Lila crosses the hall to David’s room. It’s empty, too. His blankets are thrown back near the foot of his bed, and the sheets are scattered with textbooks. His laptop is open on his desk, displaying the question, “#underline[Are you still watching?];” Clothes litter the floor. His typical mess. The neatest part of his room is the short bookshelf in the corner. He’s squeezed as much space as he can out of the shelf, ordering novels by author and series, with fantasy dominating that space. Last year’s copy of The Great American Short Story is sitting on top. It has three different bookmarks in it.

He’s not here, but it feels like he should be. This is #underline[his] place, claimed before Thrast’s magic could touch it. How can she tell that David’s room is already Associated? How is it #underline[possible] that David’s room is already Associated? Where is he? What good can a magic of meaning do on its own? It’s not like David’s stuff is radiating magic now. It’s just… #underline[his];. She can’t use Association by itself.

The Thrast-touched trees are visible outside David’s window, their branches tangled together and fused at points. She spent her childhood under and around those trees, raking up piles of leaves to play in during the fall. Now she sees, or feels, something else. It’s a strand of spider silk that aches like a sprained finger, and this sprained finger stretches out from the center of her and through the window, billowing somewhere near where she last saw Thrast. It’s coming from the part of her that has been counting the seconds since he left, but also the part of her that scurries and files through her options. It’s the part of her that reaches out for something else to hold onto. Another form of magic.

As experience becomes knowledge it comes with a flood of questions; a flurry of whats, and hows, and whys cause doubts to curl in on confusion and lock you in place. You can wind up adrift, a boat with no rudder or a strand of silk blowing in the breeze. Lila wants things, but desire is like knowledge. It leaves you flailing at everything it seems like you can reach. She wants to lash out. She wants to find her brother. She wants to plunge her fingers up through the atmosphere and reach for something higher. But how, and what first, and when to take the plunge? Magic unfurls itself to her like the blossoming fire of a wound to the chest, and it is the scroll-the-job-listings, nervous-cover-letter glance you steal before you take a dive.

This is Extension.

Standing in David’s room, with Association and Extension discovered, Lila finds her little spider-silk strand drifting through trees who share memories with David and Lila. The memories bubble free. A small child, David stands in front of a tree with their old dog Rufus. He places one hand on the tree and asks it, “Why do #underline[you] think the sun is yellow?” As a thirteen-year-old he smacks the same tree with a stick-sword. Again. Again. Rufus is barking at him.

She wanders out of David’s room. The strand of Extension that trails behind her brushes David’s bed, and she sees him there in his boxers, his puffy red eyes staring down at his hands caked with dirt and wood splinters. “David,” she hears herself whisper. He kicks the door shut.

The master bedroom is stately, in the way Lila still only vaguely recognizes as the mask of her childhood associations affecting her adult perception. Less mess, certainly, than David’s room, but little else, either. A tablet is charging on her father’s side of the bed. Her mother’s digital alarm clock is set six minutes fast to ensure she keeps herself urgent in the morning. Their clothes are hung with care. Their bathroom is pristine. As she walks the room, Lila bleeds into their things. It is almost startling, to feel herself in her parents’ place. She runs a finger along the braided silver band on her right index finger. It has five settings but only four gems.

A memory dances out of the corner of her eye.

Jenna Adamson is pressing out a skirt. She says, “This can wait. I have rounds in the morning.”

“It can #underline[wait];?” A younger Lila is incredulous. “#underline[I] have to go back to school in the morning! It’s my money, Mom. You and Dad haven’t put anything in my account for two semesters.”

“Because you asked us not to.” Jenna continues ironing.

“So it shouldn’t be big deal that I moved #underline[my] money into my own account!”

Jenna blinks. “Of course not. And I suppose you’ll be giving back my grandmother’s ring?”

“What, do you want me to?”

“If you’re so eager not to be a part of this family...”

The projected memory fades, and Lila’s clenches her fists until her knuckles crack. They will come home tonight when they’re done with whatever errands they’re running for the day. If Lila does nothing, Thrast will be waiting for them. Or worse, Lila will be waiting for them, and there will be nothing left in her but Thrast. She leaves her parents’ room and zips downstairs.

Even if Thrast, an outsider with limited understanding, less patience, and unbounded power, means what he says about trying to save the world, he can and will repeat what he did to her with others. He can’t be trusted with Earth’s present, let alone its future. But what if the magic he brought here was in another’s hands? She’s been waiting for this chance.

She pauses, staring into the kitchen. Sure enough, she glimpses a memory not fifteen minutes old, of seeing a storm building itself a body. What is Thrast, really? Probably a magic piece of jewelry he shunted his soul into to grant himself immortality, power, and protection from a righteous death. She can do that. It’s been minutes already. He could back any time. She just has to sort out the other aspects of magic first.

They still have some old winter stuff in the tote in the coat closet. Lila knows her scarf is still in there even before her Extension detects it. She gathers it out, a length of navy-colored yarn she knitted for herself, and even though it feels like her whole chest is burning she wraps the scarf around her neck. Then she returns to the garage.

Iron laces her nerves and joints. It is a constant self-declarative “Mine,” and it has been pounding through her veins since Thrast touched her mind. Lila has been using this magic her entire life, she just never realized she could do it in #underline[this direction];. Magic unfurls itself to her like the blossoming fire of a wound to the chest, and it is ownership, even of pain.

This is Domination.

With it, she can direct her strand of Extension with startling ease. Things she has already touched and Associated she can brush again with the strand without looking, but other rooms in the house are blank to her, blocked by the notion of their walls. She can also split her Extension, reaching into multiple places at once. One alights on her phone in her pocket, a panel of glass that flickers with its own light and can sense things she can’t see with her eyes. Another sets itself on the faded red jacket she left on the seat of her car.

When you’ve chosen one of the paths before you and started down that path, traveling down it wears you out. Then you rest, and eat, and you turn that time and energy into new strength. With every breath you replace exhaustion and rebuild in defiance of entropy. And when you decide that you are going to take your fragile little body to the moon, you build yourself a damn rocket. Magic unfurls itself to her like the blossoming fire of a wound to the chest, and it is the closing of the wound.

This is Edification.

Lila stands in the garage, pulling together the pieces of her gambit, when one more memory presents itself to her. It’s triggered by her staring out at her car and feeling the coming cold of night that makes her wish for her jacket. Standing there at a comfortable closeness is Montgomery Alexander Rutherford Kensington, in his perfect coat and a fresh \$200 haircut.

MARK says, “I thought I’d see if y’all would like to go out to eat, or maybe I could go grab something to bring back.”

Lila waits for the echo of her voice from the memory. It doesn’t come. She says, “It’s time to go.”

“Really? Didn’t you just get in? That’s a #underline[long] drive hun. Is your--”

Lila says, “No, babe. I don’t—I can’t--”

He reaches for her, and she pulls back. It feels so much like he could just reach forward through time and bury her in the kind of comforting hug she can’t accept if she’s going to get through this.

She remembers trying to stay gentle. “I can’t be in this anymore.”

“In this house? You #underline[are] moving out.”

“No, in #underline[this];. Us.” And then she just… stops talking. There is more to the memory, but she refuses to relive any more of it.

For all the certainty and wonder of choosing a path and following it, there are always doubts, the overwhelming questions that ring through from past indecision. Have you made the right choice? But there has to be a finality, a line drawn that you cannot cross again, or the thing you’re after may never come close enough for you to grasp it. Sometimes, you must choose to close a door. Magic unfurls itself to Lila like the blossoming fire of a wound to the chest, and it is the parting both of the teeth from the flesh and the flesh from itself.

This is Separation.

With this magic, Lila knows she can save her brother and protect her parents. She knows she can find a loose thread in Thrast and unravel him. And she knows that she can unlock the wonder of reality for the sake of Earth and all its people.

First, to hijack the power of a tyrant.

Four hair-thin feelers light on her jacket, scarf, phone and ring. She slides into the driver\'s seat of her car. Years of memories she\'s poured into her things recourse, and each of her chosen vessels Associates to her the way a loved one is wakened by a soft touch. They are each a part of her, and she categorizes them.

There are five.

Into the Oakley ring, which has passed through four generations of her family, she pours her Spirit, the essence of her emotions and connections with the world around her. A garnet, sardonyx, sapphire, and amethyst form the petals of a flower around an empty setting. The unending storm of experiences remembered, interpreted, and expected resides within Association, and through it she will assign meaning to the things she experiences. As Lila pushes her Association into the ring the empty setting is filled with a gleaming mote of fire.

And her Mind, a scramble of thought and parsed information projected into the intent of action, shunts into a smartphone wreathed in a black leather case. It is an ancillary to each other vessel: it’s not the judge but the paralegal; it is the axle not the steering wheel; it’s the statue in the mind of the sculptor; it is that which takes the measure of a divide. With her mind, she directs (but does not control) every part of herself, and from within a device that has a transceiver she can Extend herself to probe the whole of the world, if need be. While her mind’s new casing does not visibly change, its programming is irretrievably rewritten.

Her body retains her Domination. Her will, which her ring generates and her phone conducts, will be executed by her body. Lila takes a breath. She releases it.

Into her scarf she places the stuff of her vitality, the impulse of genesis present in all cells that still divide. Edification will drive her to rebuild herself and raise up both substance and structure in the world around her. Her other vessels are vectors, but Vita will be her source and her destination. She wears the scarf as the mantle of her chosen duty, to live and to support life. Its hem un-knits itself and the threads start to weave into her hair and through her shirt.

Her jacket becomes the Edge of her being, a Separation between what is Lila and what is not. It is the most essential of tools: a knife, shield, hatchet, or veil as she requires. It sits around her shoulders and takes on a strangely heavy weight.

For a moment she lingers like this, her existence divvied between five objects but still whole. The vessels carry a tension. If she releases the pressure she will bounce back into herself. It will not last on its own. There has to be a line drawn she cannot cross again. The part of her that would hesitate, that would dwell on this snap decision and weigh its ramifications, is caught up still counting seconds till Thrast’s likely return. If she is caught in the midst, she will never get the chance again. If she fails to seize this opportunity #underline[now];, while Thrast is still getting his bearings, no one on Earth will be safe from his will. So she acts.

Shifting, she slides her arms through the sleeves of her jacket and shimmies it on. A symbolic gesture, Associated instinctively with the act it accompanies: tying off each of the five vessels from the others, and snipping their connections.

Her ring flares. Her phone chirps. Her body stiffens. Her thread crawls down her arms. Then her breath escapes her lungs and her hand drops to the seat of the car, where skin and upholstery simultaneously ignite.

== He of Titles and Gifts Unutterable <he-of-titles-and-gifts-unutterable>
Thrast ascends past a commingled canopy and into the open air#footnote[It is nearly as simple as breathing. Reaching through the air with Extension, identifying the air as part of his being with Association, seizing control of it with Domination, and then the simple matter of adjusting where his body should be oriented in relation to the part of his being that was the air. This realm is too naive to know better.];. From the Lower Sky he looks out at the landscape nearby. Moments confirm much of what Lila Adamson has told him about the realm he now finds himself in.

There are many buildings, built to stand apart from each other, each with their own small field of tailored grass and even smaller aesthetic gardens. Some even have fences erected between their own plots. But each house is also connected to the others, not just by roadways but by wires that hang from poles and lattice whole communities.

He climbs higher, into the Upper Sky, and spreads his Extension farther. The tang he first sensed in the air is a corruption. It is unseasonably hot. In the distance, there are smoke-clouds in the sky from the fires of industry. Each adult seems to have a personal carriage that functions by burning a particularly flammable elixir. In one place he spots a tremendous set of rolling hills of detritus, of scrap metal and the strange substance Lila named in her mind as plastic. Fields of waste, uncared for. He finds a city of great towers mid-construction. It is swarming with people. Those that do not suffer outwardly seem to have glaring holes in their souls. They stuff this full of whatever is at hand.

“Is this what we do to ourselves,” Thrast asks himself as he hangs in the Upper Sky, “wherever we are?” As with the people of Veneth, the tide of their mistakes is coming in and they are not watching the horizon. The wind sighs around Thrast, a long deep bellow of resignation from deeply-buried hopes. “Nor will I allow this candle to snuff itself.”

He returns to the girl.

= 1.3 Caverns <caverns>
== Queen-Errant <queen-errant>
Mere emerges high on a mountainside. It is cold and windswept here, forbidding to travelers on foot. That alone is enough to shelter Kella from accidental discovery, but the seer’s other defenses are more effective. A sensation of growing dread from the suspicion that you are being watched. An intrusive line of thought, always guiding the eye down towards the relative safety of the lower slopes. Mere allows Kella these trademarks of her paranoia, but she is not dissuaded by them.

She turns against the current of Association and stomps through a half-pace of snow to a short, bristling bush. She brushes the fronds of the plant aside and ducks into the Cavern of the Seer.

“Kella,” she announces into the dark. “Why have you broken the Circle?” Before the other woman can respond Mere begins to make her way through the cave. Light like candle-flame flickers around her with no discernible source. Many branches appear, but Mere follows a straight path towards the heart of Kella’s hideaway.

The seer speaks softly, but her voice carries. “He will return, and when he does he will seek us out to punish us for our insolence.”

There is a familiar structure to Kella’s cave. It stretches on, at first without foreseeable end, while paths interpose themselves as an alternative to the only true destination available. But here in this cave, Kella does not want to be found. The distractions are her own.

“Thrast does not desire open confrontation with me,” Mere asserts.

Kella says, “Nor you him.”

“With the Circle broken we are blind, and the work is not finished.” Mere arrives, stepping lightly into a small nook that is warmed by a pile of red-hot stones.

Kella sits in her bed, wrapped up in furs and shivering. “I saw what you saw. I listened to Lenn’s game with the guardian. Our goal was never attainable.”

“We missed one piece.” Mere rests her back against the wall by the entrance. “But even for a moment we drove Thrast from our realm, and we may yet do so forevermore. We need to work quickly, and for that we must communicate.”

“Persaval has closed himself to me. Lenn may be realms away again by now. I cannot easily retrieve what was broken.”

Mere says, “I am open to your gaze, and Anshall knows he has nothing to fear from you. Connect us, at least, and we shall recover our fellows in time.”

“Can you not perform this task yourself?

“My time is strictly demanded,” Mere answers. “You are a hermit. You spend long hours in solitude only punctuated by glimpses at lands far from your mountain cave. I have stolen half an hour to speak with you in person, time I may regret having spared if you do not agree to continue our work.”

“Who is the boy?”

“A surrogate,” Mere says. “And as best I can tell an innocent.”

With no visible or audible assent, Kella’s web touches Mere and nestles into the nook that was made for it. Moments later, Anshall joins the diminished Circle. A wordless question waivers in Anshall’s connection.

“Seek out Persaval and Lenn if you wish them to be joined,” the seer says. “I cannot reconnect with the Sworn Sword without his consent, and I won’t risk reaching across realms in aimless search for the Realmshorn.”

“Thank you, Kella,” Mere says. She lets her fingers light on the woman’s forehead so that Kella can feel the coming affirmation. “You are courageous.” Then she leaves.

After she is gone Kella sits in her furs, furtively brushing a tear-track from her cheek.

\~

Time can be slippery between realms, if one is not careful. Harsh lessons have taught Mere that no amount of expediency in a realm like the Bridge can guarantee that the day she enters it is the same as the one she leaves it. So it is with the slightest of hesitation that she searches first and in vain for Lenn on the Bridge. He is not still there.

The angel is.

Mere resists the pull of the Bridge’s end, and the questions that wait for her there. With the full force of the Circle, and in the dance of their tightening noose on Thrast, it had been an acceptable risk to draw up to an observable distance, but for all her talents the Wayward Queen is not the Realmshorn, and she will not pretend to his confidence. Still, she finds that Lenn does not linger near the Sword and so she must extend her search elsewhere.

Lenn cannot return to Veneth, and Mere #underline[must not] entertain the fear that Lenn may have stumbled upon Harrows, so of the many remaining options she chooses the most challenging. It is an unpleasant place to visit, but at least it is not Harrows.

Not yet, anyway.

Mere greets a door, shadowed and susurrating, once she has dragged herself far enough out of the pull of the angel. Then, thumbing the circle of iron around the ring finger on her left hand, she plunges sword-first into the darkness.

It is not the darkness of the Seer’s cave, which comes from poorly lit surroundings and the Associative pressure of Kella’s isolation. It’s not the nothing surrounding the Bridge, which is an absence of meaning, purpose, or substance. This is a third darkness, the darkness of a place where all that exists is sound.

It is said that the Whispering Void was once a realm of splendid music, an eternal chorus creating beauty without need for other purpose. This has changed. Maybe somewhere in the void there is a pocket of the old songs, but now the realm is filled with a dreadful silence constantly encroached upon by sibilant whispers. Mere enters into this as another voice, louder and clearer than the resident voices.

“Lenn,” she says. Carried with it is all the presentation and context of physicality stripped from that physicality and folded into sound. It feels at once like the single syllable of his name and the whole statement of her purpose. #underline[I have come to this place to find and speak with Lenn, if he is here. I call you, Lenn, to trade words with me.]

He does come, but her single call eventually becomes a low and insistent song, skimming over the whispers who fill this void, and in that long unquiet wait her patience slowly frays. Other voices respond to her call, trying to cajole and propose and insinuate their way past her defenses and out of the void to realms less barren. Mere has withstood the Wolf and Wasp, so rebuffing their advances is a simple matter. Still, after long enough in the void her mind begins to stray towards the thought of what a Whisper from the Void might accomplish in the context of another realm.

And then he comes, and unlike Mere and the residents of the void he appears as himself, fully visible, his condition mocking the realm’s own structure. He is accustomed to this by now, as Mere is, but there still seems to be an itch between his shoulders that he refuses to scratch.

He positions himself as if kneeling down to brush the petals of a flower, and says to Mere, “Do you need to be so loud every time you come to this place?”

“It bothers me,” Mere says.

The whispers creep into Lenn’s silence.

“There is a boy,” she explains. “His world is at risk. But is it true he might be able to lock Thrast away?”

“Were you looking for confirmation?”

“Questions, Lenn.”

“Sorry.” He settles from his kneeling position into sitting cross-legged. “I Commanded reality to produce us someone who would choose to bear this burden for us. Declarations are leaving a bad taste in my mouth right now.”

“So that’s what you did, after you asked me to follow through. I trusted you. We all trusted you.”

“I broke the Circle,” he says, and this time she feels the honed edge of his words glistening near his own skin. Was that a product of the Void, or his Domination? “Is P—I hope that everyone is well.”

“I met with Kella,” Mere says. “I thought she broke it, out of fear. But I do not think any of us could have held it together, once I completed the curse. We are rebinding it now. If you are ready…”

Lenn holds up a hand. “Will you tell me about the boy first?”

“He has Thrast’s protections. He knew nothing of the Paradigms when he appeared. He is overcome with worry for his realm and for his family. And he is dismissive, and persistent. I left him with Persaval.”

Lenn cracks a sad smile. “How long do you think their tempers will hold?”

“I hope it will not have to be long. After I speak with Anshall I will return to them, and carry the Circle to Persaval when I reach them.”

“But not to the boy?”

“I had thought about that, but I think it would be dangerous to allow someone bound up with Thrast access to the Circle.”

There is a surprising, contemplative silence. The weight of the absence of Lenn’s voice hushes the whispers to a distant lull. Finally, he says, “You must be careful guiding the boy. If he is as willful as he sounds, an unwelcome truth revealed at the wrong moment may throw him off the path he needs to take. Do you know where his path leads?”

“The Sword.”

Lenn reaches out his hand as if for her to take it. “Remember this, my friend. It must be his choice. His will.”

Mere meets his extended hand with Association and the binding of the Circle, and the confidence and direction of the Realmshorn returns to the Circle. Kella and Anshall seem to sigh with relief.

As his parting words, Lenn says, “Shall I meet you at the end of the Bridge when the time comes?”

\~

In the moments before Mere enters the Far Halls, an aide starts, and as if unbidden by her own mind she turns toward Anshall and declaims, “Announcing her grace Mere, the Queen of Paths and Chief of Messengers, bearer of the Gilded Sword and wielder of the Iron Ring, broker of the peace of Harrows and counselor to the Far Halls.” As the aide makes the pronouncement, the open archway that marks the entrance to Anshall’s court warps, and stretches out towards a bridge shrouded by darkness. The woman claimant to the pronounced titles steps through the entryway and brushes dust from her leathers.

Mere gestures towards the aide. “Thank you.”

Anshall is a large man, prone to careful movements because of his size, and still he seems only to dangle from the throne of Kolomaer, a doll placed in a chair too large for any one person. Most of this effect is new, a weight dragging down the king’s shoulders and quelling any fire that might shine in his eyes. This man who is surrounded by the grandeur of high columns with gilding and marble, and who is attended by dozens of scribes, counselors, and adjutants, wears the countenance of someone waiting to die.

“You are, of course, welcome in my court,” Anshall says. “But must you compel the members of my court to declare your arrival?”

Mere flicks a clump of snow onto her fingers and studies it. “By your leave, is it not sacred custom in Kolomaer that visiting nobles, royals, and dignitaries be announced before entering the presence of the king or his representatives? To breach such a custom is considered a curse by your people, and I am who I am. It is Kolomaer that compels them to speak.

“I come bearing news meant for the king alone.”

Anshall nods to the members of his court. “I am happy to entertain a private audience.”

His court departs.

Mere says, “We’re not done yet.”

“Seer Aspenwood put forward a morsel of hope, to which I am happy to cling. What remains to accomplish?”

Mere crosses the hall while he speaks, until she stands before the high seat from which Anshall seems to dangle. She says, “There is a boy we believe is bound to our foe, and he may be the key to making the curse permanent. The boy is to be shepherded along a path that will fulfill the intent of our curse on our foe.”

“Am I to provide the tacit support of Kolomaer to these proceedings?”

“Your support throughout our endeavor to free Veneth has been the foundation without which we could have accomplished nothing. Do not doubt yourself, good king. There is no Circle without you.”

“Yet I sit on this throne, signing papers and sweating blood, while you tread your tireless path.”

Mere steps up beside him. “Do not get confused. I am an errand girl. You are the face of prosperity.”

“He will come for me first.”

Her thumb traces the Iron Ring. “If he returns, then yes. And that is why we will send the Sworn Sword to your side.”

Anshall’s face twists into a grimace. “That man is no shield.”

“Is there anyone else you would have stand between you and… our foe?”

“I would rather put a wall between us he can never cross.”

“He may not be a shield,” Mere offers, “but the Sword #underline[is] a wall.”

\~

The fields where she left Persaval and the boy are empty when she returns to them. She is not quite surprised by this, but it gives her pause to see a sunrise over the ocean.

She sends her irritation through the Circle. #underline[He wandered off with the boy];.

Kella responds, #underline[I can search for them, but if he does not want to be found?]

Mere orients her path towards the nearest settlement and starts walking.

= 1.4 Stormwinds <stormwinds>
== David <david>
Lake Michigan probably has a lot in common with the ocean David can’t peel his eyes off. It’s also a massive body of water. But the Lake has people who live near it, and the people and the Lake both shape and are shaped by the ways the two control each other, and while David has grown up taking day trips to the various small beaches on the shore of Lake Michigan, he has never seen a shore that is as thoroughly abandoned as this one. The Lake is a curiosity, a selling point for tourists, a destination. This ocean on Veneth is curling itself into a storm that threatens no one, except maybe for David and Mustang.

A dozen dark gray stormclouds are stacking themselves into a tower. Lightning cracks within, and the thunder that follows is distant but still rattles his chest. David expects the storm to fill the horizon, like the ocean does, but it seems more like a bear standing up to loom over a kill.

Mustang has been watching it too, or he’s watching David. Or both. “The Sea of Goletha,” he says. “There is always a storm on its waters.” When David turns, Mustang produces something from within his coat. A polished chunk of amber that gleams in the afternoon light. He stares into it and continues. “Once, the citizens of the fortress nation Goletha sailed through those storms, raiding the nations who bordered the Sea.”

David steps a little closer. Is there something moving in the amber?

Mustang tucks the chunk of amber back into his pocket. “But Goletha is gone.” Mustang starts walking toward the shore.

David keeps up. He says, “Why are you headed #underline[towards] the storm?”

“Curious?” Mustang keeps walking.

Like the sea, the field they’re walking in is, at first glance, clear of people or signs of people. David’s never been in a field this big or this abandoned, since back home most open space that’s good for growing gets used for farmland. There’s a sort of peace to tromping through the tall grass, feeling the wind on his skin. The peace feels wrong.

He puts his hands in his pockets, and in one he discovers his phone. Mustang isn’t saying anything else, and it looks like they’re a long way from anywhere, so he pulls it out. It says he has no service. Shocker. But he can still get to his voicemail? He puts his phone to his ear to listen to a message from this morning.

“Hey. I got laid off. On my way home for the weekend.” She’s probably home now, stumbling into Veneth’s bogeyman. Lila says, “Haven’t told Mom and Dad yet. Let me break it them, okay?”

Mustang doesn’t see him messing with his phone. David opens his most recent text messages.

DAD

- I think we’re out of pickles

  - Thumbs up emoji

- Did you just type out “thumbs up emoji”

  - :P

David rolls his eyes again.

MARK

- Can you tell your folks thanks again for dinner the other night?

- You paid for everything

  - Not for the company and conversation

- Okay Ill mention it

- Hey can you not show up this weekend

  - ?

- Lila’s gonna be in

- So stay away and be cool?

  - Did you finish filling out that application I sent you?

- Dude I told you she’s not gonna take you back just cuz yur trying to help me get a job

  - But if I get you a job, get this, you’ll have a job

  - \>:D

- I don’t need a job man I’ve got time

- Just give her space okay

David’s foot catches on something he can’t see in the grass. There is a crack when it hits. He stumbles, wheeling sideways and almost dropping his phone, but he catches his balance again. The wind blows. His foot doesn’t hurt. His shoe isn’t even scuffed. Instead, the large gray block of stone he kicked has a little notch near the corner. He squats in the tall grass.

The block is part of something larger that is hidden by the grass growing around it. The grass doesn’t grow inside, which he would have noticed sooner if he was paying attention. There is a parking lot’s worth of huge stone bricks stretching out from the block he’s kicked. Some patches have been punched through by especially stubborn grass, and in other places weeds, moss, and wildflowers are doing the work of reclaiming it. David steps over the brick and into the lot.

“Hey Mustang!” he calls. “What was this?” He wanders towards a collapsed arch at the far end of the lot. Mustang doesn’t say anything at first; he just turns and cuts back towards David. There are symbols on the arch and carved into the stone at the base of it. Past the arch there’s nothing but grass. One entire side of the arch has fallen down, but somehow the crown of the arch is still standing free. It has a picture of a sword with its tip pointed down, like it’s ready to plunge straight through the symbol cut into the stone of the lot underneath it. David steps a little closer.

“Do not step through!” Mustang hisses. He is suddenly, surprisingly, close enough to snatch David’s arm and draw him back.

“How is that still hanging there?”

“The stone may be crumbling, but the gate still functions.”

“So this is a magic gate?”

“It is a Marshall’s Gate, once used to transport troops across great distances.”

“So it’s a war gate.”

“Yes,” Mustang says finally. “But it is damaged, and the other side may not still be intact.”

“Why did people stop using them?”

Mustang takes a distancing step from the gate. “They left.” He points into the distance, away from the shore. David follows Mustang’s finger and sees nothing. But he keeps looking, while Mustang continues to point, and eventually he spots the scattered and mostly overgrown bones of a road that leads out to the distant edges of the field.

He says, “This was Qastra. Up that long road was a fortress full of soldiers who would march down to use this gate when aid was called for.”

“Was,” David repeats. “What did he do to them?”

With a hollow tone, Mustang says, “He found them useful.”

David groans. “Nope, don’t like that.”

“Do you start to see?”

“Yeah, I see. I’m not letting you off the hook, but I understand why you tried what you did.”

“You do not have me ‘on a hook,’” Mustang insists.

David thinks Yeah but he has me on one but keeps that to himself. Instead he explains, “I’m holding you responsible for your mistake.”

“I will shoulder that weight as well.” Mustang’s eyes lift to the sword depicted on the arch. “Stay close to me, David Adamson, and I will be a shield.”

David says, “It doesn’t seem like I need a shield.”

Mustang spins and starts back towards the shoreline and the storm.

Before long, they reach a path like the road Mustang pointed out near the gate. Mustang leads them along it. The storm gathering over the sea is reaching out with a harsh wind that stirs the grasses they walk between.

“It’s been a couple hours,” David says. “Unless time travels differently between worlds.”

“There is still time before sunset.”

“But in the meantime your eldritch Napoleon is having a blast reshaping the island of Elba.”

“I assume your reference was coherent in your mother tongue.”

“A bold assumption,” David says. “I know it really hasn’t been long, as far as long walks go, but don’t you have some way to take us places faster?”

“Do you want to go back and see where the Marshall’s Gate takes you?”

David decides to change topics. “What was that amber for, when you were talking about the sea?”

Mustang continues to walk in silence, but he matches David’s pace. Eventually, his words weighed, he says, “Riverfolk believe amber catches memories of the past and holds them as it forms. Because we believe this, when Riverfolk peer into amber we can see the history the amber has preserved. It is stronger in Ea’river.”

“That’s awesome.”

“Thrast used that belief to become the Prophet of History, and twisted my entire nation to his will.”

“Never mind that sucks. Is that what he does to everything?”

Mustang doesn’t say he’s sorry, but the tension in his shoulders carries more than naked apologies. “Yes.”

“You used his name.”

“If he is truly gone from Veneth, then I cannot draw his attention by speaking his name. And with Mere gone, it is easier to close us off from anyone who might spy on us.”

“But you still won’t tell me your name?”

“You’ve chosen another that seems to serve you well enough.”

“A nickname’s not a real name, man.”

“All names are real, but their influence varies.”

“So you’re gonna answer to Mustang?”

“I did. I will. But I have sworn no oaths by that name. It has no ties by blood or bond with which to sway me, except for what you and I have exchanged.”

“What kinds of oaths have you sworn?”

“Service,” Mustang says; he straightens further when he says it, “and honor.”

“To?”

“Ea’river.”

“So you’re a rogue cop.”

“I am the last Sworn Sword of Ea’river.”

“I don’t think a club with no sharp edges is a sword.”

“Dermike is an instrument. #underline[I] am the sword.”

“And shield, apparently.”

“Separation can serve both purposes at once.”

“What’s your other one? You said you have two.”

“Association, as is common on Veneth. Without other Paradigms, or as one who is isolated from others, Association is almost useless. But I have found uses for it.”

“Is that the names thing?”

“And the fire.”

“Fire?”

“Association is bound up with each person’s essence, and the essence of a life is commonly viewed as a flame: death is like snuffing out a candle, and a beloved person illuminates a room for others. Emotions are often felt as heat within the body. Because of this, what gives you meaning and purpose, that which kindles your passion, can be drawn out from associations into actual flame by those so Aligned.”

“I gotta say, a lot of this Paradigm stuff is sounding like tricks of the light.”

“Is it not so where you are from? How much of your world is dictated by perspective? How often do perspectives win out that shelter those who hold them from seeing the harm caused by their actions? Light is what it is, and we must choose where and how to shine it.”

“I guess I shouldn’t expect anything else from a guy who wants to go by ‘Arbiter.’ So have you figured out what went wrong yet?”

Mustang pauses and sniffs the air. He holds one hand out from his side and buries his fingers in the grass, feeling the wind play the blades across his fingers. He says, “My part in the curse was to supply the chisel that would part Thrast from Veneth. In keeping myself protected while I supplied that chisel, I was not party to the way Mere swung the hammer, or which bucket was selected for the shavings to fall. Mere left to gather the others and the knowledge we lacked.”

“So someone knows?”

“He might,” Mustang says. “But I will not know for sure unless Mere brings word from him. He and I are Separated.”

“Who is ‘he?’”

“I think you would like him.” Mustang starts up the path again. He’s moving faster. “He is also fond of questions.”

“Wait a minute. Is something happening?”

“As you said, it is a long walk.”

“But there is something?”

“Just keep up.” And he starts to run.

The abandoned road winds its way to the shore over the course of a handful of miles. Mustang doesn’t take the path in a sprint mostly because he has to keep stopping to wait for David. David is surprised to learn that, while he seems to be immune to swords and stubbed toes, he’s still vulnerable to being out of shape and out of breath. Five minutes into trailing after Mustang has him slowing and gasping for air and rubbing at his burning muscles.

“Normally I could help you,” Mustang says, the second time he is forced to stop and wait while David pants, “but the barrier that prevents you from harm also prevents me from separating you from your pain or lightening you enough for me to carry you on my shoulders.”

“Can’t you separate yourself from my weight and carry me that way?”

“Your condition is obstinate.”

“What’s the point of protecting me from harm if I can just kill myself from exhaustion?”

“If I am to guess, it is to allow your body to grow stronger from exertion.”

“Great.”

David loses track of the sun. He loses track of his thoughts a couple times, between pauses for breath. He’s certain that Mustang is going to give up and carry him eventually, but exhaustion never seems to overtake him. It just #underline[hurts];.

Every time they stop, the wind seems to be blowing harder, and the thunder that shakes David’s chest gets closer and closer to shaking him down to his knees.

“Got anything else to say about why we’re now #underline[running] #underline[to] the nightmare storm?”

Mustang lifts him by the arm. “There’s a village. If you can talk you can breathe.”

They run. The storm rises up into the sky like Zeus is getting ready to smite just this one specific village. The grass is gradually choked out with stones and sand. Mustang grows increasingly urgent, but he never leaves David behind. David can’t decide if Mustang is concerned for him or waiting for him to zombify. David’s legs and side and lungs protest, but slowly they seem to accept the new state of suffering. He takes it one breath and three stumbling steps at a time.

It does help that when he falls he doesn’t get scratched, and that the pounding headaches he sometimes gets when he overworks never come. Maybe he can get used to this. Maybe it will help him get in shape.

Finally, the village comes into view. It is no larger than David imagined, just a small handful of thatched huts on a hill overlooking a beach with several boats that have been dragged ashore and lashed to something that might hold them in place. There are no people visible outside the huts, and David hopes there aren’t any people inside the huts either, because the proximity of the storm is destroying his balance. It feels more like the beach and the path are on the surface of a satellite hanging over the actual planet, the storm; any second David and Mustang and the village are going to lose their grip on the surface and plummet into the ire that waits below. But David asserts to himself that the storm is neither #underline[up] nor #underline[down];, it’s #underline[over there];, and no matter how close and overwhelming it is the storm hasn’t upended reality (because something else has already done that).

When Mustang catches sight of the village, he breaks into a true sprint, stopping for neither David or sure footing until he is standing in the center of its little gathering of huts. David stumbles after, feeling a wave of something press past him and enclose the village like a bubble. The wind dies down. Mustang stands with his hands outstretched, his face dark and scrunched together.

Someone steps out of her home, peering out to see why the world has suddenly stilled. She looks between Mustang and the storm a few times. Then, her head turns sharply and she disappears back into her hut. David glances in the direction her head turns and he wishes he had a tent flap he could hide behind.

At the far end of the fishing village the hill the village rests on rises again, and there’s part of a stone building sticking out of that rise with a huge pair of doors closing the entrance to a cave. The ponderous doors of the barrow have slammed open, and a man has emerged, clad in the armor he was buried in and holding a gleaming sword out towards Mustang. His torn and rotting flesh is knitting itself back together, and regaining a healthy color, but the look of hatred in his eyes only deepens.

Without the screaming wind to drown it out, the soldier’s words carry to David’s ears ever-so-clearly. “So long as there is need on Veneth for a righteous blade, the Shining Legion will rise to meet that need!” He marches out of the barrow’s doorway, and there are four other soldiers following him. They, too, are healing from decades of time among the dead. “Persaval of Ea’river, you have broken your oath to the Lord Commander of the Legion and carved out the very Soul of Veneth! Stand, traitor, and answer for your sin!”

David feels his knees lock up. That hook in his brain tugs, sending a wave of nausea through him. He keels over into the dirt.

Mustang doesn’t respond.

The revenant soldiers form up outside the barrows. With one voice they cry, “Riverman, you stand accused! Speak your answer!”

Without turning his eyes from the storm, Mustang tells David, “This is what he does. In his fervor to prevent the ruin of all things, he has undone Veneth one nation at a time.”

“Blasphemy!” scream the soldiers. They charge.

Mustang’s look of concentration doesn’t change, but he does pull his not-sword out. It licks with fire as it’s drawing free, and the tongues of the fire leap to the growing and curl around the feet of the charging soldiers. Then small flickers become an instant bonfire, engulfing all of the soldiers in the flames.

The screams are terrible.

Persaval takes a deep breath. He turns away from the storm and to the soldiers, who were only slowed by his fire. They pick their way out of it, covering in hideous burns that are already blistering and falling away to be replaced by fresh pink skin underneath. One of the soldier startles as he rises again, his almost glowing eyes shifting off of Persaval and then past him. At David.

The soldier screams again, as if he still on fire. “What have you done to the Lord Commander? Why do you wear his graces?”

David stammers, and a wave of fire knocks the soldiers back again. But now the attention of all five soldiers is on David. Their glares seem to dig through his eyes and scramble for the knot tied to him. His heart squeezes itself up into his throat.

And then David is gone.

= 1.5 Instant Metapsychic Split <instant-metapsychic-split>
== Lila <lila-1>
Lila is, to no one’s surprise, not prepared for the consequences of an instant metapsychic split.

When Annabeth Oakley was presented with what her descendants would call the Oakley ring it was set with a modestly-sized garnet. Annabeth then gave it to her daughter Rebecca, represented by a sardonyx of similar size. Rebecca added a sapphire for Jenna, but she also had another setting prepared when she did, so that when Jenna handed the ring down to Lila she was compelled both to fill a setting with Lila’s amethyst and prepare another empty one, where the gem from Lila’s daughter would continue the tradition. Like Rebecca, Jenna never discussed this expectation with her daughter. She only gave Lila the ring and said, “My mother gave me this when I came of age. Now I’m giving it to you.”

In lieu of the expected precious stone the eye of the flower is a small, gleaming ember. Within the ring the slurry of fear and anger that is panic spills into a chasm that is her devastation at the results of her gambit, and a dozen other lesser but still stringent colors of passion contribute to the blackbody radiation the band of the Oakley ring is now emitting in the visible spectrum. Thus, the inside of Lila’s car is on fire.

How does a mind work after it has been crammed into a smartphone? Synapses are traded for chip-boards. Compartmental schema contort into the shapes of apps and functions. Sensory input from her body is set aside in favor of the receiver, accepting packets of data borne on light-waves for her to parse.

The parts of Lila that evaluate information, sort it, and enable her to act on it are cut off from her. She cut them off. So the vessel from which she’d planned to conduct her newly partitioned life instead leaves her mind wrestling in streams of light she lacks the perspective to understand. And while the mind wrestles, the flames of decentralized panic only grow hotter.

Separated from mind and spirit to govern the engine of her will, the body that houses her Domination lies dormant. Without the essence of life to fuel the engine and foster those infinitesimal deeds of her cells necessary to continuously maintain everything that makes an organism alive (firing synapses, respiration, circulation, cell division, biochemical combustion…), Lila is, simply, #underline[dead];.

Her homespun scarf spins more of itself, seeking life to foster and chaos to bring to order. But without models to follow, paths to take, or the will to #underline[do];, the threads, like sprouts, can only grow towards a source of energy. Most, then, spread too close to the ring and are consumed by the rising flames in the cabin of the car, but in doing so they provide a buffer between her corpse and self-immolation.

The buffer will not last forever. The hand that wears the Oakley ring is already a smoldering husk, and the heat is crawling up to consume more of her arm. A magnificent intent means nothing if you lack the will to take it up.

If the Oakley ring is her spirit inflamed, her phone is her scattering mind, her body is her dormant flesh suit, and her scarf is aimless Vita, then what is or will become of the jacket with which she made her disastrous division? It is the scalpel dropped between the tray and the table; it is the walls of a tower built without doors or windows. Yes, the same lines drawn to Separate her vessels are keeping much of the heat of her spirit from consuming her other selves, but the heat licks through unmanned gaps in those walls. Her Edge should have written a comma between “I will partition myself” and “and then I will,” but instead it punched a period.

No, Lila was not ready. But her spirit is not bound by her former arrangement, and the exothermic consequences of that are only passive. #underline[Lila] inhabits the Oakley ring, and she is free to compose:

#quote(block: true)[
  That’s not how it is

  Lila is not an essay

  She is poetry

  Lady on Fire paint yourself wearing five faces

  Mind tick to airplane, listen it stills cacophony

  Will and direction meet in her body wakening

  Life that’s untangled kindles the breath of industry

  Severed selves seize your liberty, tear the car apart
]

Lila shambles away from the ruptured shell of her burning Saturn and out into the driveway. Her feet scrape the asphalt, then stiffen. She takes in a breath like water trickling through a blocked drain.

#quote(block: true)[
  What fires wrought this heart?

  This woman now disjointed

  What will she be like?

  Why did we make Lila

  A concept to work toward

  Are we building something here

  Is this breath I’m taking

  The first or the last

  You made a hard choice

  Mind the path it takes

  Walk it while you can

  Build and not tear down

  What step do I take

  Next?

  Align my selves, assign my mind the throne

  I’ll be the voice behind the president

  My arms will carry out what we decide

  The thread I weave will keep us all alive

  And Edge will line the bones of precedent
]

Lila holds her breath. She chose the responsibility to sustain life. It’s best she starts with herself.

Her spirit’s excess heat is siphoned away at her mind’s direction and by her will expressed in extending her arm and spreading cracked and blackened fingers. The heat from the ring is converted into energy to serve Vita in repairing her body. Edge starves the flames and excises flesh that can’t be rescued, clearing the way for Vita’s threads to weave a sleeve of fabric all the way down to her wrist. Her hand remains a skeletal husk as a reminder to her selves of what happens when she plays with fire.

Lila exhales. It’s a start. As her panic recedes, the flare of heat powering her healing recedes to a steady flicker. Her spirit finds this good, and her mind seeks to make that useful.

She proceeds with turning her turmoil into quanta of healing: electrical signals and chemical energy. She misses things. The brain is now mostly a tool, and one already damaged by the shock of her transformation. The heart pumps, but the production of white blood cells is forgotten. The bacteria in her gut and the acid in her stomach have not stopped working, but with less fuel for Vita she finds the gestalt of her separate pieces sending fingers of blended magic to pick out food in her digestive system and transmute it directly into the fuel of her spirit.

Fingers of magic that start within her stretch out loose from her body, snapping up asphalt and grass and vapor in the air and burning it as fuel to kickstart a new semblance of life. She recalls the sight of Thrast in the kitchen, claiming everything in sight as part of a new body. It occurs to her how easily he could have just taken the wealth of resources bound up in her body. It occurs to her that, during these moment of scrambling to recreate something recognizable out of her selves, she might not have the self-control to stop her selves from doing just that to any passers-by.

As if summoned by her thinking of him, Thrast returns. She feels him first, as she lets careful feelers of Extension stretch out from her. He descends to the end of the driveway as an armored nimbus of hastily manufactured flesh. His feet touch the asphalt and Lila looks him dead in his false eyes. Fury flares. Intent coils in her like a snake. As her withering gaze washes over him, before she can act, Thrast’s body collapses into a heap of ashes, and where it stood there is David.

There is no warning of this.

David sees Lila in the old worn jacket Mom bought her in high school and her homemade scarf thrown loose around her shoulders. Her hair is singed. One arm is tucked into her jacket out of sight. Her feet are scarcely touching the ground. She is sick-pale, not just white-girl-pale.

She sees David, yes, but first she feels him with a finger of Extension that had reached out to Thrast. Waves of her Association with him pulse out from her ring, and travel the line that connects them to find that magic has already laid hands on David. He carries a bundle, knotted around and into his soul. The bundle might as well have the name #emph[Thrast] printed on the side in bold letters. Despite this knowledge, her eyes see only her brother, face contorting with alarm and horror.

“I’m back,” he says. He says, “Oh dear God I’m back.”

= Part Two: Extension <part-two-extension>
= 2.1 Back <back>
== David <david-1>
“David?” Lila asks. There is an unusual delay between impetus and speech. At first, it almost comes out of her speakers.

“What did he do to you?” he asks, before taking a closer look. He comes near to her.

Her baffled amusement is a flare of heat from her ring, which she turns into a strained chuckle. “That was my question.”

“No. Li. You look like hell.”

“Which circle?”

“The cold one.”

“You look all right,” Lila observes. “Smell a bit like soot.”

David spots the car, with its ruined door. “Do I wanna know what the house looks like?”

“Stay out of the kitchen,” she says, then, “actually, you should probably just go.”

“Leave?” David starts to reach for her, but the strand of Extension she still has laid on his heart pulses with Separation from her coat, and he doesn’t know why he lowers his hand. “Go because of what came here?”

Lila makes her head nod.

David shakes his head. “If I have it right, you won’t see him as long as I’m around. They mangled the spell to banish him and made us switch places instead. Apparently it didn’t stick.”

“That means you’ve been… somewhere else.”

“Yeah.”

David had been staring at the car, but now he is tracing the path of molten metal and char marks to where Lila is standing. He meets her eyes. “What did he do? You can tell me.”

Lila withers from the question. “Do you think he’s coming back?”

“I don’t see why not, Li. It’s happened twice, without warning and for no reason I can see. Whatever they did must have stamped us on two sides of a coin and sent it spinning.”

“He says he wants to fix Earth.”

“He what?”

Lila’s lips get lazy. Maybe she hopes David won’t notice, but her mouth stops synching up with the words she speaks. “He asked me what was wrong with this world, and showed me a shattered planet, and said he’d stop at nothing to prevent the ruin he foresaw.”

David shivers, though he doesn’t really feel an emotion tied to it. It’s a reaction his body has, to some thought triggered by what Lila says. “World-ending monsters with messiah complexes. The best.” He pauses, trying to give his sister a bit longer to answer his actual question. Then, “I don’t think he can be hurt.”

“No,” Lila agrees. “He’s a lich.”

“And people on the other side are afraid to give their names because of what it might let him to do them.”

“He can reach into your mind and take over your body,” Lila says.

For a split second, David rears back. But no, he knows that this woman before him, however sick, is his sister. Then, his alarm simmers into a cold rage. He asks, “Why did he let you go?”

“I think he didn’t want to kill me to learn what I know.” A radiance pours from inside her coat. “His mistake.”

He is upset for her, but her attitude is worrying. “Li.”

The look on her face is unfamiliar. He’s seen her angry, and this is not how it looks. Usually her eyes turn to ice. Now, though, her eyes are as almost-vacant as they’ve been since he returned. But he can feel the shift in the atmosphere that always came with her rage, and this time it is not just a series of unconscious signals. It is a wave of heat that blisters the air.

She stops hiding her hand.

The flesh is burned almost to the bone, and the ring she has worn since seventh grade rages white-hot. The air around the ring steams.

David’s hands are frozen, half-stretched towards his sister. She is not the one who stopped them this time. He asks, “What happened to you?”

“I’m working on it.”

“That’s not an answer.”

“I don’t have a good one.”

David’s voice strains. “Try.”

“Thrast brought magic here. When he used his magic on me, I woke to it. I’m still figuring it out, but--”

“Look at your hand!”

“I know.”

“I knew I was going to be too late.”

“David. There is a man with unimaginable power running amok on two worlds, and #emph[#underline[his philosophy is bad];];. We have to do something about it.”

“No, #underline[I] have to do something about it. I’m the one tied up in this. You could have just backed away and stayed safe.”

“You know,” Lila says, “that I would never back away from something like this.”

“Yeah I do know. Now look at you.”

“And what are you going to do?”

David takes out his phone. “I don’t even get to look him in the eyes.” He swipes to his contacts, taps a name. “Tell me he doesn’t look like me.”

“Fortunately for him.” Lila’s voice is flat.

His mom picks up. “Hi honey.”

“Mom,” David says. “Don’t come home.”

“Sorry, I think we’re driving through some bad coverage. What was that?”

“Don’t come home. Don’t led Dad come home. Get a hotel and wait. Don’t look for me. Don’t look for Lila.”

“David Huxley, I’m not paying for your phone service so you can--”

“Not joking, mom. It’s bad. The house isn’t safe. I’ll be in touch. Just don’t look for us.”

“You don’t get to take that tone with me,” Jenna Adamson says over the phone. “Stop scaring your mother and tell me what’s going on right now.”

“There’s a dangerous criminal on the loose. He’s taking hostages.”

“Oh my God what.”

“I love you.”

David hangs up. He is looking Lila in the eyes for the whole conversation. It’s like looking at a doll, but one that moves. One that tries to put together a few words and fails before he hangs up.

“You know that’s not going to work,” Lila says.

David turns towards the road. “Yeah let’s get the hell away from the house.”

