Kyle and Jenna Adamson arrive home in defiance of David’s request, and they find the wreckage of their daughter’s car.

They call everyone. Emergency services. Their neighbors. Their children. Answers are not forthcoming.

A sluggish dance of panic unravels the afternoon. Kyle books a hotel.

= 2.2 No Circle Can Be Perfect <no-circle-can-be-perfect>
== Watcher <watcher>
After Mere leaves her, Kella turns her attentions to Veneth at large. Her first curiosity, and simplest task, is to tenderly probe a world no longer held by the throat.

Ea’river’s cities have not noticed Thrast’s absence. What few servants Thrast retains who are Aligned with a Paradigm are in some ways emptier husks than most of those who toil in his directive to “elevate” the peoples of Veneth to his standards. They go about their appointed tasks with the diligence of beasts of burden, and even in his absence Thrast’s influence on them is too strong for Kella to find purchase without overextending herself. And she is, as she must be, very careful about how far she reaches.

The Gentlefishers sail turbulent seas, untouched by the storms, and carry food and material between the nations as ever they have. Kella passes them by. There is nothing to gain from them, or their approach to life.

Qastra is barren, haunted only by the remnants of the Legion and its grounds. Here, too, Kella must be careful, because what life remains in Qastra is wholly consumed by Thrast’s intent. #underline[Except];, as Kella finds in her brief survey, there is an unaligned fishing village that has appeared on a Qastran shoreline, braving the possibility of detection.

The Far Halls of Kolomaer, across the sea, thrive with Anshall on his seat. Its people have thus far evaded Thrast’s Domination, in no small part because of the many trade agreements, brokered peaces, and relative harmlessness of the kingdom. Thrast’s attentions, however, were beginning to turn there, so Anshall’s anxiety radiates out into his subjects, fostering in them a growing dread.

Goletha is empty, no longer even haunted by those who once lived within. It has been generations since Goletha’s impenetrable fortresses were cleared of their occupants. Where did Thrast send them? How did he take their permanence upon himself? Questions to which Kella does not have the time or talent to find the answers.

While Kella feels her way through Veneth, the queen travels other paths, probing a network of realms that Kella hesitates to observe even at distance. Mere will find Lenn out there in the winds of reality, but it will take time. During that time, what can Kella do but watch and wait? Well, one thing.

Kella reaches out to Anshall. He is in anguish, even after the relief of the Circle’s rebinding. The closest members of his court suffer with him, not knowing the reason for his turmoil but expressing silent sympathy nonetheless. There are business transactions and other matters of state to sign off on, but his aides delegate most of these away, and the most pressing are delayed, without being afforded a chance to burden Anshall’s mind. It is the way of the Far Halls, a sign of affection for the face of Kolomaer. It is a small comfort, while Anshall waits to die. But Thrast does not descend, and in time he notices Kella Aspenwood’s touch.

Once he becomes aware of their contact, she asks, #emph[#underline[Are you still out of reach, Anshall];];?

He murmurs too softly for the members of his court to hear. “Kella, I am as distant from you as I will ever be.”

On impulse, as ever, she sends a surge of the deepest warmth to him, her unspoken secret exposed as a tender-flower-beating-heart gift for him. He breathes it in, as he always does, but neither Anshall nor Kella can take the risk to join in truth. Neither can he reciprocate, except to increase what love she already bears him.

She feels that love, not his but a gift from him nonetheless, and fills herself with it. It may seem a false hope offered as a consolation, but Kella has delved through its layers on many lonely nights: he is a physical man, and she is a distant presence; both of their Alignments are necessary to sustain the Circle, so coming together is too great a risk to the whole; if Lenn and Persaval must remain apart for the sake of all Veneth and the structure of reality, then even a mutual pining seems an act of disrespect. So he stokes her love, for which she longs but also on which she thrives.

Kella tells him they have failed. There is a boy who has taken Thrast’s place on Veneth, and the boy’s home is doomed to suffer as all Veneth has under the wizard’s scrutiny. But, and she offers this as a plea to Anshall, there is a thread of hope. A game of questions Lost Lenn played with an angel.

“You would have me pull on that thread.”

#emph[#underline[Weave it as you will, my distant king.];]

Even on Veneth, where belief holds sway over reality, it is difficult for even a master Aligned with Edification to foster the growth of a concept, especially one as tenuous as hope. But he begins to dig into it, seizing on the bonds of the Circle and guiding Kella’s far-reaching minds towards the knowledge he needs to lift hope high. It lifts his head to the surface of the water, and sustains him for the agonizing time he must spend waiting to be visited by the Wayward Queen.

Having shared what she can, she withdraws from him.

While Thrast is gone, Veneth quivers. Only after spreading herself out over the realm does she really feel it. Thrast has burrowed his way into the bones of the world and anchored himself there. Those anchors hold despite the curse. There is another, somewhere hidden from Kella by Persaval’s Separation, who is bound to those anchors. She wonders again what will happen if those anchors are ripped free and there is nothing to take their place? How will Veneth move, when it is freed from Thrast?

Wondering this, over the course of hours and the passing of a night, makes her increasingly anxious. Where is this boy through whom their hopes are threaded? Can she find him, despite the Sword Sword?

Then Mere returns from Kolomaer and echoes Kella’s curiosity with a frustrated observation, #underline[He wandered off with the boy];. When Mere returns, and utters this, Kella feels Veneth stop quivering. She swallows her fear and assures the queen that she will search, but now she is not sure she wants to find Persaval.

She starts from where Mere is now. A field, not far from where Kella thought she found the fishing village on the shores of Qastra. When Mere and Persaval used the Bridge to escape from Thrast’s tower, they must have come out there. Perhaps she could predict where, but Persaval hides himself with Separation and even blocks out her ability to detect certain things around him. This is why she did not know where the three went after departing Thrast’s tower, and it is why she could not find him until now.

But the village she thought she had found on the Qastran shores is gone. Or, when she tries to look for it, she cannot feel it. She warns Mere that something might be wrong, but Mere is not deterred.

Slowly, achingly, Veneth begins to hum, and as it does Kella Aspenwood retreats. Fear is a motivator, yes, but also wisdom. The peerless will of the grand wizard Thrast is clawing its way free from Persaval’s cage of Separation, and the bonds of the Circle must not be caught in his grip.

= 2.3 What Fires Wrought This Heart <what-fires-wrought-this-heart>
== Thrast? <thrast>
Being reduced to an object with no sensory organs is like being a mind floating in a void. Thrast\'s alignment with each paradigm does little to deter the feeling. With Association and Extension he knows that his colophon is sitting on a pile of ash in what was once a pasture. A man is standing over him. He knows that the man is Persaval, Sworn Sword and Arbiter of Ea\'river-that-was. He knows that he is surrounded by an aura of Separation directed by Persaval\'s malice. He knows that the air is hot and hostile to life. He knows that the sea is two leagues away. But knowledge is not sight. Knowledge is not tasting the air, or hearing the crackle of flames around him.

While he dwells on this, Thrast digs his Extension into the dirt, spreading a wide network by which he can affect soil the Sworn Sword is not suppressing.

“How long will you continue in this way?” Thrast’s voice is a rumble in the earth. “Committing wanton destruction in the name of your hatred?”

“Wanton destruction?” Persaval muses. He murmurs, below the dull roar of flames. He is untouchable, a being scarcely perceived beyond the wall of his will. “Heedless pursuit of a singular goal, regardless of consequences? You describe yourself.”

“I rebuild what I have brought low. I seek opportunities for restoration, and a renewal of purpose. You pass judgment, and leave the ashes to the wind.”

“When there is a blight in the orchard, you cannot stop at separating the rotten fruit. You must cut the rot back to the source.”

Thrast begins to mold a humanoid form half a dozen paces beneath Persaval’s feet. To mask this, he says, “Your attempt to remove me from your orchard has failed because you do not recognize that I am the heart of the orchard. I am Veneth. Without me, this world will wither into ruin.”

“Then I will find an unblighted seed, and shelter it till it grows anew.”

“You are not a shield,” Thrast reminds Persaval. Only half-bidden, the wind brushes around them like a great sigh. “You are a blade.” Thrast moves the doll of earth towards the coast as he shapes it. He gives it eyes to see and a mouth to speak.

“Why do you think you will remain on Veneth?” Persaval demands. “What makes you think we have failed?”

“You meant to banish me. I am not banished.”

“You are breaking the terms of your exile.”

“I did not consent to exile.”

“You set yourself apart from us,” Persaval says, “like you are something beyond humanity.”

A gust of wind scoops up the ash piled beneath Thrast’s colophon, briefly suggesting the shape of a person gesturing toward itself. A pressure wave and blast of flame shreds the impression.

Two leagues away, a wave of murky sand rises up to crash against an incoming swell. When the waves of water and sand recede, a statue stands where they broke against each other. Gravel lids scrape open, dousing a pair of mirrors with sunlight. The statue descends to one knee so that water embraces its waist.

“Am I not?” Thrast’s voice echoes on the wind.

= 2.4 Running <running>
David

In many places, the Kalamazoo River is surrounded by ravine that was carved back when the river was a thing elemental in nature and not just an involuntary repository for the waste products of human civilization. If you don\'t look at the river directly it can be stunning at times, a snapshot of the river dragging generations of trees into its murk.

David picks his way along a deer path, several steps ahead of Lila. “The first thing they did when I showed up was try to kill me,” he says. “They thought I was him, playing a trick.”

“I tried to kill him first, too,” Lila says. “He\'s the kind of bad you don\'t play games with.”

There is a raspberry bush half-buried by leaves. David bends and plucks a berry. It tastes just fine. “They said he took over their country.”

“That\'s probably his strategy for ‘fixing things.’”

“Something to watch out for,” he says. “when we’re not scrambling to keep him from killing everyone we love.”

“He won’t target Mom and Dad.”

“But we’re in this, and that means they won’t stay out of it.”

“How long do you think we have?”

“It’s been, what, an hour?” David pushes through a pricklebush. He only notices because a barb catches in his shirt. Lila brushes past it, and the plant withers. “So any second now, probably.”

“And we’re just going to hide until then?”

David tromps. “#underline[I] am making distance between Thrast and anywhere important, because, and this is only if I actually understand what’s happening, he’ll come back wherever I am and so making distance is the only thing I can do to him, short of maybe locking myself in a faraday cage.”

“This isn’t electromagnetic trickery,” Lila assures him, with words put together in binary and parsed back into English. The emergency alert system overrides her preferences and delivers an amber alert. “This is metaphysical hacking.”

“So the people I met on the other side were talking about alignments and paradigms,” David says. “They said I should be ‘aligned with all five.’”

“There must be five.”

“The way you said that’s not at all disturbing.”

“You looking for answers or just chatting?”

“I don’t know. Looking for a diplomatic way to repeat ‘what the hell did you do to yourself?’”

“I divided my being into five parts.”

“But WHY?” He turns, his whole head flushing hot.

Spirit flares in Lila’s ring, answering the question in elemental meaning. A line of code reviews itself in her phone, analyzing the actions she took and providing probable causes. Her body tenses her muscles, flexing a level of control she has not yet known. Then, carefully, she tells her brother the truth. “It was a ring in a puddle in a cave, and I won the riddle.”

“I am not gonna have the argument right now about what if Boromir found the ring instead of Bilbo.”

“Arwen.”

“Galadriel? Wait. Stop. Listen. What is your actual vision here, Li?”

“I\'m going to figure it out.”

“\'It’ is?”

“Whatever it needs to be.”

David looks around him. “Okay. Okay.”

Silence falls over wild places when humans intrude, but the stillness now is deeper than that. Lila is out of place, with an arm made of yarn and a ring of fire on her hand. Part of her is questing through the woods, and David can see now the vine here and bush there that have crumbled into ashes. He doesn\'t challenge her on that. There isn\'t time. They\'re maybe two miles from the road. If he could\'ve snagged a car they might have gotten farther from home, but this is where they got.

“Okay,” he says. “We should split up. If he can\'t find you, maybe you can figure yourself out, do some good.” He doesn\'t voice his plea that she\'s planning to do good with…. “Hide, if you can? I\'m gonna go as nuts as I can to screw him up.”

He turns toward the river.

“David? Are we good?”

“We\'re alive, aren\'t we?”

At first his descent is careful, despite himself. When you spend nineteen years of your life learning how to steward your body, you don’t unlearn that care quickly--unless something traumatic shears off your need for self-preservation. But David reminds himself he could only have seconds, and maybe momentum is conserved when you blink across realities, so he picks up his pace and summarily loses his balance. He keels forward. His head cracks against a root but he feels no pain. He doesn’t see the root splinter because he is too busy completing the first of a dozen or so involuntary somersaults. With every impact he anticipates a complaint from his nerves that never comes. It is a painless, loud, clumsy descent from the slope into the river.

It is the Kalamazoo river in mid-August. It hasn’t rained for about two weeks. Here, a couple miles from his parents’ house, the river runs lazy. He is not hurried off to some other place. But David #underline[is] dragged under by the current, and it pushes him down. Down towards the riverbed. Downstream. Down, into muck that obscures the sun even from an arm’s length beneath the surface.

His eyes are wide open, and dirt and the water don’t so much as sting them. He can faintly feel the place where an invisible barrier stops water from running into his ears or nose. He waits for his lungs to start burning, but they never do.

There, in the murk that drags him along, he feels no physical discomfort. But the dark starts to look a little bit like nothing. And maybe, in the midst of that nothing, he can glimpse dull gray stonework stretching out to either end of oblivion.

