After David runs off into the river, Lila switches Airplane Mode off.

The breath of the world brushes her fingertips, but it comes in packets, like a printing ribbon transcribing pixels to a page one line at a time. Missed calls. Tweet notifications. Queuing advertisements. A game update. All the powers of distraction possessed by modern technology flood the scattered thoughts of a half-embodied mind. She turns her attention from the world around to the screen she used to stare at from the outside. She dismisses notifications, rejects the download, and sifts through her contacts, looking for David. The screen flickers in her pocket.

- Hey

It’s MARK, from half an hour ago. There are several more from fifteen minutes ago.

- Your mom texted me

- u okay?

- I want u to be

- if that counts for anything

Lila flits into David’s contact information and Shares herself to his phone, because there is no point to diving head first into magic if she doesn’t use it to stay close to those she loves. Rapidly, if piecemeal, she feels her awareness Extend to David’s phone. Most of the device is locked away from her. She can’t read his texts, find out how good his service is, or screen his calls. But there is the slightest approximation of the sense of movement, and there is the constant, overwhelming, Thrast-stamped magic that suffuses David. She considers texting him from inside his own phone, but he is preoccupied with other things at the moment.

There is a current carrying David away, and not just the literal Kalamazoo river. Part of it is the momentum of some magic that has cords wrapped around both David and Thrast. Part of it is David’s own mind, taking a slow turn from one avenue of thought to another. As the fingers of her Extension draw near this current (but cannot touch it, no matter how close they come) Lila can almost glimpse the monolith that looms before her brother.

Then David is gone.

== Watcher <watcher-1>
Kella trembles, and peers as if through her fingers, while Thrast wiles his way out of Persaval’s grip. She is curled up in a bundle, her head covered by the thickest of her blankets, and still she cannot look way. This is where he will break loose and track them down, starting with the most vulnerable. This is where the Circle will be unmade more completely than it was ever forged. This is the end.

A new body of living stone surges out into the Bay of Qastra. As it hurtles beneath the waves, it turns gradually towards the surface. Above, the sea roils into a hideous storm, all roaring swells, booming thunder, and crackling bolts. A Paradigmatic masterwork, terrible in its finality, is rising with Thrast’s body, and it will break on the Circle the moment Thrast breaks the surface of the deep. She feels Mere and Persaval brace themselves, and Anshall simultaneously flinching and building up their reserves of strength. All she can do is watch, horrified, as Thrast turns his will on them.

But it is not Thrast who emerges, but a boy wrapped in Thrast’s trappings, and bearing in his pocket something else entirely. The storm’s ire loses its focuses, instead dissipating into a vast but natural chaos which carries David Huxley Adamson swiftly out into the sea of Goletha.

Kella wraps her blankets tight around her body and sobs herself senseless.

