Stormclouds are split by the prow of a ship, where a figurehead carved into the shape of a sailor is holding an iron lantern. The lantern spreads a calm on the sea nearby, stilling the waves beneath the ship but leaving a strong breeze behind it.

The sailors of this ship fish a strange boy out of the water. His clothes are of a foreign make and he is startlingly pale, but both garments and body are well-preserved. When he is settled on the deck, he shoves a small, buzzing device into a pocket in his trousers and rests against the side of the ship.

He says, “Thanks for the rescue. Being lost at sea is exhausting.” He is completely unharmed.

In keeping with custom most of the sailors return to their duties; they leave their Second to interrogate their new passenger. The Second is a smallish man, though his body has the same appearance of dark leather over hard fiber as the rest of the crew. His face has the look of a stone that washes up on a beach after a few hundred years at the mercy of the waves. A pair of golden shackles hang down around his neck, clinking unclasped across his chest as he crouches down in front of the strange salvage.

The Second says to David, “We know no other ships in these waters.”

“Neither do I.”

“We are far from the shore.”

“I knew it! I haven’t seen land all day.”

Presented with these deflections, the Second’s face remains as it must have been all those years beneath the surface. He says, “If you are not cast-away, or from an island we do not know, then where did you come from? Hm? I think a storm-caller or a gate-skipper tricks you. Yes?”

“I’m not out here making deals with devils,” David answers. “I just can’t seem to travel point A to point B by way of a line.”

“But magic sits with you.” The Second shifts, and the sunlight flashes off the cuffs around his neck. “I must learn what our hospitality will cost us, yes? I am he who pays it.”

They are a piece of art, these shackles. The curve of each link in their chain is engraved with intertwining lines. The cuffs themselves are stamped with a simple image, one David knows more certainly than he should is of the sun hanging over the horizon. This stamp is on the inside, where it would press into the skin of someone who was made to wear them. And sure enough, there is a raised scar on the inside of each of the Second’s wrists, depicting the same sun and horizon.

To David’s scrutinty the Second says, “It is for freedom.” Then he continues to press, “How do I loosen your tongue, mm? Are you hungry?”

“What do you eat?”

“We have salt—”

David sits forward. “Hi, no, I said ‘what do #underline[you] eat?’”

“—salt-cured fish and brined fruit.”

David sits back again. When he tilts his head back, he can see the storm still raging outside the eerie calm that radiates from the figurehead’s lantern. “Do you have enough to spare?”

The Second says again, “I am to learn what our hospitality costs.”

“And pay it,” David says. He steels himself and looks into the Second’s eyes. They are kind. Kind eyes in an almost featureless face. Now to spoil it. “I am an albatross. Do you have those?”

“Have one now, mm?”

“Okay.” He sighs and tries again, “I’m a bad omen. At any moment I could be gone, and Thrast will be in my place.”

The Second slowly stands. “We go out of our way to keep peace. Why make threats, mm?”

“It’s not a threat. It’s an apology. I don’t know how to stop it.” They have fished him out of the water, but he’s still driftwood.

“If you die, mm? Does he lose this opening?”

David groans up onto his knees. When he moves, the Second takes a step back. David says, “Lot of ways to pay prices, huh? Try if you want, but the lady with the gilded sword stabbed me and it didn’t break skin. You could throw me back into the sea, but I’ll just bob around until some other crew of fine fellows picks me up or Thrast switches places with me again.”

The Second walks away. He catches one of the other sailors, and quietly leads the man over to look on David. The Second mutters in the sailor’s ear, and then they have a quick, wide-eyed conversation under their breaths. The sailor says something definitive. The Second nods as the sailor returns to his business. He repeats this process with every sailor on the ship. Then he stands a few yards clear of David, watching him with that expressionless face, while the sun falls behind the stormclouds outside the range of the ship’s lantern. Finally, the Second reapproaches.

“I know our wishes now, yes?” he says, his face inches from David’s. “We will not try to kill you today.”

Despite having convinced himself he had no reason to worry about it, David feels relief rattle loose in his chest.

The Second says, “And how could we give you to the sea, hm? Our lantern sheds light on peace. Peace is a kind woman, yes? Even to an albatross.”

“Thank you,” David says. “But can I do something for you?”

“Can you, hm? What skills do you have, albatross?”

“That might be less important than that I can’t seem to be hurt. Do you have any dangerous jobs you could give to someone expendable?”

By dark, David has fallen from the masts three times. The first time is intentional, to prove to himself and the sailors that a fall won’t hurt him, let alone kill him. He has also replaced a couple fraying ropes and scrubbed a particularly stubborn section of the deck. He is tired and sore, but the work proves a fine distraction from the anxiety that seeks to build in him with every moment he remains in one tire-tread of reality. When he is finished, he collapses into a heap below-decks and fishes out his phone.

He expects there to be hundreds of messages, given Phone-Lila’s chattiness and then the constant buzzing while he was lost at sea, but there is only one waiting for him when he unlocks the screen.

