“What is this, mm?” A sailor casts a shadow over David. He is gesturing to David’s phone.

David sets it down between his legs. “It lets me write to my sister.”

The man’s drops himself down next to him. He has a ceramic jar full of something alcoholic dashed with citrus. So the lantern apparently doesn’t magically prevent scurvy. “I see, hm?”

David takes his phone and scrolls up the conversation, then shows the sailor some of his back and forth with Lila. “I didn’t expect it to work this far from home,” he tells the sailor.

“She writes back, yes?”

“She does.”

“With one of these?”

“Yeah.” David omits that in this case it happens to be with the same phone he’s using. “Where I’m from almost everyone has one of these.”

The sailor smiles. “It must give great comfort, to have your loved ones so close. Hard to stay lonely, hm?”

“Sometimes,” David says, quietly, “it’s torture. You can reach out to them, but you can’t touch them.”

The sailor hums. “We Gentlefishers have this trouble too, yes? Sometimes a little force looks the best course. But we hold peace to heart, and Veneth, she makes the spirit flesh.”

“Are you afraid of magic?”

A quizzical look.

“With all that Thrast can do, with all that he has done to this world, isn’t it frightening to consider the tools he uses to make it all happen? That for every lantern that radiates peace there might be an unkillable killer?”

“Do I fear the sea because it storms, mm? Me, I am afraid of dogs and carving knives. Is your sister well?”

“I’m worried about her,” David says. “She doesn’t want me to worry. She wants me to stay out of her way so she can deal with the problem herself.”

“Problem?”

David says, “Thrast. He’s attacking my home, and she insists that she should be the one to defend it.”

“Defend from Thrast, mm? Others have tried.”

“Will you?” David asks quietly. “When he switches places with me here?”

“You say ‘when,’ Albatross.”

“It’s happened three times already, and I can’t sort out what causes it.”

“When it happens again, you will see your sister, yes?”

“Maybe.”

“The best path to peace, yes? It is by looking forward to what is good.”

David’s eyebrows try to meet on the bridge of his nose. “I’m sorry, no, I don’t think I can sit back and hope a solution into existence.”

“Did I say to take only what you are given, mm? Tell me, yes? How would you get to the deck from here?”

“The stairs are right over there.”

“All the details.”

“I would stand up and walk over to and up the stairs.”

“If there is a broken plank in your path?”

“I’d step over it.”

“A wall between you?”

“Walk around it.”

“And a man with a blade blocking your way?”

David pauses.

The sailor continues, “You say, ‘I am going up those stairs to look at the sky.’ He says, ‘You do not deserve the sky, Albatross.’ And you say?” The sailor waits, expectant. “Mm? You say, ‘But #underline[you] deserve it.’”

“And then he stabs me,” David says.

“Say he does, yes? And his blade bounces off like soggy loaf of bread. And you say again, ‘I am going up those stairs to look at the sky.’ Then you say, ‘Will you come look with me?’”

David says, “He does come with me, and then suddenly I’m back home and there’s Thrast, and then sword-guy is dead and I am a world away from the deck of your ship.”

The sailor sighs. “When Thrast comes, we will invite him onto our deck to look at the sky. If he will not come, then we will do better convincing.”

David wipes moisture from his eyes. “What is he actually like? When I’m whisked off, what can I expect is going to happen to anyone too close?”

“This was Goletha’s sea, yes? Long before I was born, there were no Gentlefishers in this sea. The ships of Goletha sank any other ships that came to their waters. Now Goletha is gone. Five cities and all their walled highways emptied of people. There is only one Citizen of Goletha left.

“Does he have a reason?”

“Me, I think he wants everything #underline[right];, and trusts no one else to make it so.”

David glances at his phone again. “Do you guys have somewhere for me to sleep? I think I have to try or I’m going to be useless in the morning.”

“I will have my Second prepare you a cot,” the sailor says, and stands.

“Your Second? So are you the… First?”

“We are all first, Albatross,” the sailor answers. “Except the Second, mm? He serves up his freedom for all our sakes.”

David settles back. “Thrast is going to hate you.”

“Thrast does not hate, yes? I think he is not human enough to do that anymore.”

