== Lila <lila-2>
There are, it turns out, several ways to fly.

If you lean on Separation, as Lila does when she first tries to chase down Thrast, it’s all about pushing the ground (and then the trees) away from you. This generates something that looks, from the outside, like a pressure wave, as a force like a wingbeat or a jet engine thrusts leaves and grasses aside. As effective and impressive as it is for getting you into the air, though, it does not give you much control over the direction at which you and the ground are separating. While rocketing up through the canopy, Lila brushes one branch with her ringed hand. It immediately cracks from the sudden heat and the bark peels away. She extends a bubble of Separation from her jacket. Several more branches are broken when they are pushed away from her body. Then she is through, and a glare half-blinds her as she tries to trace which way Thrast fled. In focusing on her search she forgets the restraining order she has placed on the ground to keep it no less than thirty feet away from her, and has to scramble to catch herselves.

In her scramble, she considers the following alternate methods of flight: divorcing herselves from Earth’s gravity (she rejects this because it would actually just send her hurtling off into space); Associating her density and the air with the ratio for a human body and water (she doesn’t feel like swimming); setting “down” as somewhere closer to the horizon (she’ll save that for when she decides where she’s going). Instead, she settles on convincing her body that there is a pillar jutting up through the trees on which she can stand#footnote[This is surprisingly easy? All she has to do is draw the shape with Extension, set it to #emph[Interacts with Lila’s body];, define its borders with Separation, and force her body to obey the hacked version of physical reality with Domination.];. The pillar is only half-visible, but it fully does the job of standing between her and a plummet back to the ground. The problem of falling solved, she can get back to hunting. Lila opens Navigation and quickly has a map of her location (this is off by a mile or two, obviously, because she’s not on a road). She searches “towns near me,” and finds that yes, #underline[damn it];, Kalamazoo is just 10 miles away as the clay-man flies.

She tries to make the center of Kalamazoo “down,” but it is impossible. The Earth is an ovoid sphere weighing 6 sextillion tons. In what scenario does that mass not supersede any other inputs into the equation without also causing everything in sight to tear itself apart? But again, if she makes Earth’s gravity stop working on her then she will stop orbiting the planet quickly. If she assigns something else, like Kalamazoo, for her to gravitate towards, the pull will either be too weak to match the velocity at which Earth is orbiting the sun or so strong that she might rip up the city.

Thrast doesn’t seem to have this problem. But then Thrast probably doesn’t have Sir Isaac Newton sitting him down in front of a blackboard and quietly lecturing him about the laws of motion, and he certainly doesn’t have Einstein and Hubble leaning against the doorway waiting for their turn. His ignorance leaves him free to Associate reality by whatever logic makes sense to him.

Lila will just have to figure out what works for her.

While she calculates the trajectory she’ll need to launch herselves to Kalamazoo, she stretches a tight-knit yarn catwalk out in front of her, supported on more half-real pillars, and starts walking. She doesn’t get past the first step of the function, however, before MARK calls her.

