Lila chooses to conserve energy. She gathers up yarn from behind her as she goes, wrapping it like a giant’s blanket around her shoulders. Some of it she lays back in front of her as a platform, but she saves more of it for the descent. After hanging up with MARK she finishes tracking her path, and continues until her yarn catwalk is hanging over the river. Then, she coils into a squat atop a half-real pillar, as if this is going to be a leap and not unleashing ordnance. She fashions a half-real nose cone, pretends it’s part of an invisible jet with her inside, and deploys her Separation rocket.

The river beneath her explodes, but Lila doesn’t notice. She is too busy trying to keep her body conscious while it experiences upwards of 20 Gs. The cone helps keep her from spiraling in the wind, but the gyroscopic sensor in her phone loses it, her ears pop, and she starts to smell toasted almonds from the first time she rode in an airplane. Her mind, with a degree of Separation from her bodily experience, is aware that her selves are doing as she intended, hurtling up into the air in a long arc that will carry her to the city of Kalamazoo in a minute’s time. She also concludes this stunt would probably have killed her if she had not Separated her selves.

As her body approaches the height of her arc a full fifteen seconds after the assisted leap, her acceleration slows and it becomes easier to track what is happening to her. The gyroscope in her phone settles. Her knowledge about her position and trajectory are no longer abstract; she is streaking through the air somewhere well above terminal velocity for a human-sized object, but now she can see Kalamazoo ahead, a modest skyline interspersed with trees and surrounded by the River. Ten seconds pass, and she crests her jump and starts to fall. Her cone does a fair enough job cutting through air resistance to maintain her forward momentum.

She thinks she might reach the city in time, and if she doesn’t give Thrast time to react maybe she can shatter his clay shell into pieces before he can do whatever it is he plans to with the city and its people. She is still considering how she might do that at fifty seconds into her jump, as she lets loose the yarn from her shoulders to slow her descent, and it is what occupies her thoughts when, six seconds from when she should be landing in the outskirts of Kalamazoo, she detects the barrier. There is no time, traveling at somewhere around 900 feet a second, for her to react.

Her body crashes off an invisible wall surrounding the city and clips off the railway of a bridge on its way into the river.

