Attempting to destroy Thrast’s vessel is not working, but what remains of Lila insists that it will. Lila’s ring, the seat of her Spirit, radiates like a miniature sun, boiling the world around her with hate, pain, and frustration. Thrast is not surprised by the force of it. This is the woman, after all, whose first reaction to seeing him was to try to kill him. He is, however, pushed back, having to maintain tautologies of Separation and assert his identity as the Citizen of Goletha to keep her from Associating him directly into a pile of ash. Thankfully, she has no supernatural means to reach out for him. She does, however, have a body.

It leaves him no quarter. Lila’s body, damaged as it is, is a vessel for unfiltered Domination. Thrast puppets Mr. Kensington along rooftops, moving the young man’s body with preternatural grace and a disdain for true physicality. Lila, on the other hand, moves like a beast all sinew and teeth, every motion shrinking the gap between them. Mr. Kensington’s breath quickly runs ragged, as his body responds naturally to the experience of being hunted. Thrast can feel the pressure of that will tightening on his center. With the force of Spirit and Will, she seeks to peel off all Thrast’s defenses. He has faced this before, caught in a Game with a master of Domination, and that knowledge gives him an idea.

Mr. Kensington still has an avenue into Lila Adamson’s identity, and Thrast uses it to communicate. He shows her a different man wearing the amber star, one cloaked in wolf’s fur and surrounded by weapons and bodies. He shows her that man’s lover, clad in white and blazing with Associative fire not so unlike Lila’s own. He shows how heedless the lover’s assault, how passionately he fought to free his love from Thrast’s grip. He shows, more, the glimpse of ruin that was tied to the man in wolf’s fur, and hopes that some lingering reason in her will see why he had to ensure that fang would be pulled for the sake of Veneth.

While he shows her these things, he sidesteps and ducks away and gives ground.

One of his new thralls screams and falls silent below, sending a tremor through his network of Earthly Associations. There is nothing left of that one but ash, and even those ashes are atomized into fuel for Lila’s hunt. No, that will not do at all. He cannot allow this woman to waste priceless human souls in her heedless assault. He retreats, causing Mr. Kensington to sail into the air and towards a forest, miles from the city, where she can do less harm to those she pretends she is avenging.

“#underline[I’ve found a foothold];,” lies Montgomery Kensington. “#underline[It’s only a matter of time before I take back control];.” Thrast has checked, and Mr. Kensington is still sealed off in his tomb, powerless#footnote[He is, as foretold, louder than before.];.

It grows difficult to account for much apart from Lila. Her presence builds on itself, subsuming matter and the flows of energy, and manufacturing instances of her self that are only revealed to be false when he directs his full attention towards them. She weaves the two of them in together, not needing Extension to bind them up because Thrast controls something that belongs to her, and because he cannot help but have allowed this confrontation between them to escalate both in intensity and intimacy. Not only is Lila making dolls of thread in the image of her body, but she is making more selves. That one is woven from golden strands of fire as well as yarn. This has a gap where its throat should be. Thrast recognizes this, as well. He has done something similar back on Veneth.

He shows her the many minds he has made his own. He shows how each individual is given value in working against the Breaking That Looms. He presses, for he knows in his soul that what she seeks to destroy is not distinct from the methods she is currently using to pursue that destruction. #underline[We are the same];, he means, #underline[but you can be better than this];.

Better? The question rings in trees that are blowing in the wind one moment and then, with one last tremor, fall still. It is in the moisture that siphons from the brush and soil beneath their feet. The Associative fire gradually stills, replaced by a growing chill. Laughter in a peal of thunder trembles with another question. Is this Thrast?

He knows that she is probing, exerting her will that he simply not exist, but who he is makes him strong. Stronger than #underline[her];. If she so desires this lesson, then he will teach it.

(As he begins, Mr. Kensington whispers in his ear, “I belong to Someone who is mightier than you.”)

I was born to a traveling merchant while she camped on holy ground, and for that I was chosen to be a prophet, who could see and safeguard the future of Veneth. I aligned with Association at birth, and since then I have become bound out with my world’s fate. I am the Prophet Thrast, the Soul of Veneth.

(That doesn’t exist.)

I am the sole remnant of the nation of Goletha, a nation that shall not fall. I am Thrast, the Citizen of Goletha.

(There is no such place.)

I am Lord Commander of Qastra’s Shining Legion.

(Who?)

I am the Overseer of Ea’river Ambered Histories, the Builder of Bridges, the Master of Every Sea. I have seen the end of my world and I have been blessed with the knowledge and power necessary to prevent it.

(No.)

I am the Tower of Kalamazoo, the Bringer of Magic, the Finder of All Meaning, and Savior of the Earth.

#underline[You are nothing];.

One of his titles shivers, and then crumbles away. Master of Every Sea? Can he yet name three of the seas on this world he claims to be Savior of? No, such a title means nothing on Earth. Now Thrast is ringed in, with pieces of his selfhood plinking free of this shell in the flash freeze of Lila’s disdain. He lacks the advantage he had over Persaval on Veneth, because this is #underline[not] his beloved home, and so much of this Earth has yet to be Associated with Thrast. More, he is bound to a new vessel, and—wait.

Mr. Kensington says, “You kept me in a box.”

At the same time, Lila too says, “You kept him in a box.”

“You assigned yourself to me, and thought you cleverest trick was that we were both things caged. You will stay in our cage until you let me free.”

Thrast says, through Mr. Kensington’s mouth, “I am not one who can be restrained by bonds.”

“You,” Lila insists, “are #underline[nothing];.”

== David <david-10>
There is a realm nestled near the Bridge, a wyrd amalgam creeping like a vine from realm to realm, a thing that stifles and siphons from every thing it touches. This realm is known to itself and the few minds that retain knowledge of its existence as Harrows. It is an endless, insatiable sprint of a reality, and it is hungry.

Some time before any soul with the misfortune to have encountered them can still remember, the Wolf and the Wasp have been lying in wait for opportunities to extend the borders of Harrows. They murmur, reverberate, and stretch across the realm’s twisted expanses, an ever burgeoning presence in a world of howling winds, circuitous lightning and indistinct whispers. They are Harrows’ North Wind.

It has been some time since Harrows has been able to engulf another realm, and the need to extend itself has not dulled with time. When the Queen re-enters the Bridge and considers her past with Harrows, the Wolf and the Wasp take note, and they press in. There is another mind with her, capable of his own connections and unsuspecting. Even as the Queen dismisses Harrows from her mind, the Wolf and the Wasp open the realm to that second mind, allowing him to see whatever it is he wants to see.

He takes the bait.

The moment he steps onto the desiccated branch of a giant, long-dead tree, a feeling settles around him. It is like a great jaw has unhinged and surrounded him with teeth. He cannot see the teeth, except the shadows at the corners of his vision. There is a buzz, smaller than the sense of the jaws, but whizzing over and around and under him, an impatient, angry warning. The Wolf and the Wasp breathe deep of an unclaimed mortal’s presence in their realm, gathering more about this boy than he would ever permit, if he had the fortitude to shield himself.

His name is David Huxley Adamson; it is a fascinating name#footnote[The Boy King of An Inhospitable Place, Heir to the Firstborn.];, though of course he does not realize, as its Associations have been diluted by centuries of mundane context. He is from a realm called Earth (it is vulnerable to encroachment), and he is tangled up with a creature of great power, whom he hates. He is warded from all harm; he believes this to be a property he gets from his entanglement with the creature he knows as Thrast; he also believes that this will keep him safe. He is strong-willed, cautious, and refuses to allow himself to come into his power. And, perhaps most significant of all, he is grieving the loss of one he loves. There is another mind, folded up in the tangles of David and Thrast, another small being woven in with one of the boy’s effects. She repulses their intrusion: she is a world her own, and David has shut her away with his grief. If it is possible to imagine, the looming jaws of the Wolf curve into a smile. Yes. He will serve.

The lifeless branch beneath David’s feet cracks and then grows vital, extending its drooping length out into a path for him to walk. Words are spoken, not quite through sound, and he understands them. “To whom may we offer our thanks, for the new life you have brought to this land?” The buzzing swirls around him again, sweetening into a crystalline ringing, then zips off down the path laid out for David.

He takes a halting step forward. His face is turned down in a grimace, and despite his confidence in the safety of his body he does not relish the thought of falling off this branch. Unlike the Bridge, this place is cluttered. Lightning flashes sideways, tracing the path of the crystalline voice. A yellowish mist permeates the air beyond the regrowing branch. When the branch comes in contact with the mist, the mist clears to a pale blue glitter that hangs in the air. Here, he can breathe. Here, there are footfalls and thunder. He weighs this path against what he expected, and then against the lies Mere was telling him, and he chooses to go on.

He does not answer the voice of the Wolf and the Wasp.

He follows the branch to the end. As he goes, his presence appears to awaken the landscape. New, smaller branches sprout from his pathway, growing great green leaves and sometimes fruit the size of his head. The fog of the sky clears into a stunning view of a sunrise. Its light stings his eyes, golden and heavy, towing the hope of a new day towards the sky. Eventually, the great limb beneath him tapers to a point, but it has stretched itself out onto massive, aged marble steps. He pauses before stepping off. A song like chimes in the wind reaches his ears, and it asks him, “Grim savior, what burdens your bright heart?”

A furrow of the brow. His eyes dart, catching some shadow that shifts in the branches beneath him. He swears there is something reaching out to touch him. Something like fingers, trying to brush aside the veil of Thrast’s magic and lay a comforting hand on his very soul. The sense sets his body tingling, but he can’t tell if it’s fear or anticipation. He tells himself, “’I am going up those stairs to look at the sky.’”

He follows the stairs to their height. His steps herald colors, like painted light, filling in cracks in the marble. As he reaches the top of the stair, the whole realm seems to sigh in relief. From this height, he can see a sprawling stone city, empty and swathed in sunlight. The Wolf and the Wasp say, “Lifebringer, do you not know she yet lives?”

David closes his eyes. The sunlight shines red through his eyelids. “Don’t mock me.”

In response, the red light that filters through his lids dims to a near gray. A breeze brushes through his hair. No, it is a hot breath, heavy with the smell of a dog’s spit. The voice tells him, “You can save her, if you are quick.” When he opens his eyes again, the stone city is one of metal and asphalt.

“What do you want from me?”

“#underline[From] you?” whispers Harrows. The crystal song separates out into a harsh buzz once more. The words, however, remain honeyed. “We seek only to aid you who have been anointed. We have access to many worlds. Perhaps we can help you reach your own in time.”

David wrestles. Clearly, the voice cannot be trusted. But its offer is magnetic. Below, the Earth city sprouts black spires. The spires spray gouts of fire that linger, bouncing through the streets and then coming to orbit their spires again. He can almost make out a distant sound of screaming. He turns back the way he came. The branch has withered out of sight. Its dry, blackening leaves bluster in the winds of the realm, first towards him and then away, as if carried on the breath of an unseen monster.

“How can we serve each other, Firstborn?”

David thinks, #underline[I am not the firstborn];. He pulls out his phone.

- Hey I’m here in hell and the devil says you’re not dead

“What is that?”

- Alive or not, I’m coming for you

“Why can’t we touch it?”

- You remember what Dad says? Doesn’t matter how far away we move, or what we screwed up this time

- We’re part of the same family, and that means we’re always home

David reaches into that sentiment and pulls out its heart, and finds a path home through the corniest possible circumstance. He Facetimes his sister.

== Mr. Kensington <mr.-kensington>
Thrast is panicking. It’s tangible, even locked away like MARK is. This is not a man who thought he could ever be backed into a corner. But Thrast is also not a man to surrender to his fear. A truth is acknowledged, which resonates through MARK’s being: Thrast cannot safely remain on Earth. Following this truth, there is a resolution, and this is where MARK becomes lost.

Thrast makes the body of Mr. Kensington curl up into a ball on the forest floor while he thinks. It doesn’t take him long to think, once he’s made up his mind.

There’s an answer to Lila’s assertion that Thrast is nothing from nowhere. It is twofold. First, Thrast remembers Veneth to exist, and he trusts his memory. Second, there is a person who is tangled up with Thrast, becoming increasingly apparent to him, and that person’s experience confirms Thrast’s belief that he is from Veneth.

The one who confirms Thrast also allows Thrast to discern why he has been shifting between Veneth and Earth: they are trading places. So there must be a Paradigmatic method for realm transfer (also confirmed by the existence of Mere, the “banishment” he has been experiencing, and how he was able to get rid of Lenn). This has evaded him since he discovered there were other realms because of how closely he has been tied to Veneth, but now he is not there, and Lila is taking a knife to the ties he formed to that place (and to his very existence).

All he really needs to accomplish the task at hand is to reject this place as Lila is rejecting Veneth, and reach through the person at the other end of his entanglements to touch Veneth. Once there, he can re-gather himself, eliminate the threats to his home, and, when he is ready, return to Earth and rescue it from the destruction Lila is sure to bring to it.

So he does. Mr. Kensington stands, and extends a hand out toward an imagined space beyond the bounds of Lila’s Separation. There is a sensation, like a suction cup peeling off his face mixed with a deep, terrible tearing. Then, with a slight gasp of air, MARK collapses, and the amber star spills off from around his neck.

Then Thrast switches places with David.

= 3.5 The Only Game There Is <the-only-game-there-is>
== Realmshorn <realmshorn>
“Where has he gone?” Lenn’s question to Mere echoes throughout the Bridge, chasing out David’s silence.

Lenn knows nothing so well as he knows the measure of a will. From the unwitting way that David has tossed around the nature of the Bridge, ushering in absolute silence and hammering out its already thin line, Lenn knows that the boy on whom Veneth’s hopes are pinned has a will like an obsidian knife. Lethally sharp, but brittle. If the danger presented to David comes to bear, it will take every ounce of Lenn’s skill to maintain the path to victory.

When he feels a Game coming, especially one that weighs as heavy as the one he feels now, Lenn prepares himself. Slipping into the state of mind used to take him hours, or days. Now, can he even tell the shift is coming?

“I want us to be clear.” Is Mere trembling? “The boy has stepped into the deepest mire I know, and you will risk your very selfhood to retrieve him.”

Is Lenn concerned for himself? “To whom do I belong?”~

Is there an answer, however subtle, in the bond of the Circle? Every gesture from Persaval is faint when he feels them at all, no matter how Anshall strains to rebuild their connection. This is for the best. If he caught Persaval’s scent again, what realm would Lenn not bend beneath his will to reach his husband?

Does Mere sense the ache beneath? “He has gone to Harrows. I believe he is the first to enter since I bound it against invading the Bridge. I have told you how that place is pernicious. Thrast is a usurper and a tyrant. Harrows is #underline[hungry];.”

“Would we rather be cattle or prey?”

“If we must be one or the other, choose cattle.”

“Should I remain with the Circle?”

“Now that #underline[he] has rejoined the Circle, and while I am on the Bridge, that path is closed to Harrows.” Does Mere trust her own assurance? Her brow is dark, and her gaze is distant. Is she haunted, or merely focuses?

“Any more advice?”

“Friend, be well.”

Does Mere command a door to appear, or is it that she permits it to open? Is it even the same act of will Lenn imagines of himself? Some paths taken are currents and not roads. Does Mere #underline[decide];, or #underline[discover];?

Is this door a mirror? It shows itself as one from his memory: a door cobbled from leftovers. The top hinge kept coming loose, but what did they even need a door for? They only closed it when there was a fire going. Is this reflection meant to entice him? Just the sight of it triggers the smell of soot.

Why would a hunter lay such a clumsy trap? Is it so full of hunger and malice that it would just as happily mock its prey as to trick it? Does it lack the discipline to control its impulses?

Does that make it any less dangerous?

Is a door just a door, or is it the shape of the division when a will separates itself from a place? If so, then is this door Mere’s, Harrows’, or Lenn’s? To whom does a door belong? Is it the maker, the occupant behind the barrier, or the one who can open it? And what about the space beyond? If someone controls the door through which to access a space, do they not also control the space itself?

What is it to be the Realmshorn? Is it a curse to be unbound to spaces or the wills that inhabit them? Or is it a skeleton key? Will Lenn always be like a lamb with no wool, able to slip into tighter crevasses without snagging on their hazards, or will time, his flighty companion, one day see him regrow his belonging?

Can it see him out here, this place called Harrows? Does it feel his gaze on the gate to its being? Is it slavering? Is it shuddering? Is it curling its paw around the meal it already captured?

How does one pass through a door when one knows it to be false? This time, Lenn drifts through like smoke through a window. Despite his framing, there is a sharp transition when he passes through the closed teeth of Mere’s brokered peace and leaves the Bridge. All the reflective menace glimpsed behind a mocking mirror shatters into the #underline[presence] of a realm. But is all this noise, the shifting gases and crackling husks, what composes the dreaded Harrows? Is this intended to impress? Do these tattered veils really fool the poor souls who stumble on this shambles of a realm?~

Is this chaos for him? Does the realm itself tremble when the Realmshorn enters its borders? Does it know better than to disregard him? Is it shoring up its defenses and scrambling its paths all in anticipation of his coming?

Where is the boy? How might this place have tried to deceive one such as David? Flattery? Veiled menace? Mockery? And how would he respond, stubborn and fragile as he is? Would he run? And what might he knock loose in his need to get away?

Is it better to proceed in silence, or to provoke an answer? Lenn calls out, “David?”

Is there fear in Harrows’ silence? Is there arrogance? Can it afford either mistake?

Are these paths meant to confound him? Lenn steps out onto a road that feints at crumbling. It attempts to wend through itself, but he turns a half step and leaps out into murk. The murk resolves into a branch on which he lights but does not linger. The branch withers and snaps, but he shifts his weight and angles his fall into a dive with hand outstretched. A vine is caught, and Lenn is on level ground again before it can snap.

Did this place begin with Extension? Are these careening diversions roots from an ancient tree? Or is that simply a trace of another of its victims, lost to time? What makes Harrows tick? Is it hunger, as Mere says? Or is there some other joint that twisted early, and made it what it is?

Why are so many of the realms that touch the Bridge sundered, or twisted, or faded? Is it Harrows, reaching out to them? Is Harrows just another realm jarred into conflict with its first purpose? If there was a time, however brief, when all the realms were whole, what did that look like? What wonders were accomplished?

Did anything happen at all?

Is that David? It’s the first figure Lenn has seen that wasn’t suggested by husk-like leaves blown in the wind or a haphazard shadow. A young man crests a hill, wearing a Gentlefisher’s jacket and with his hands buried in deep pockets in his trousers. He is looking out over a vista. What is he considering that the tumult of Harrows shapes itself around him in one moment like the jaws of a wolf and in another like the mane of a lion?

Does it matter? Lenn glimpses him, takes in enough to be confident it is who he seeks, and then the boy is gone, replaced by an amber star in a pendant that suspends itself in the air. Thrast. Is this what Mere feared? Then Lenn must be quick. He approaches with steps and turns like a dance, and as he does he asks Thrast a question.

“Have you considered your course?” Normally, it is best to force your opponent to ask the first question, but Lenn has already lost a contest with Thrast once. Trusting Mere’s word, he drags on the Circle’s strength and binds this Game, if it happens, to the breaths before Thrast finishes whatever effect will thrust the wizard out of Harrows.

“What pestilence did my counterpart mire himself in?” Thrast asks, prompting Lenn to relax. Thrast will play the Game.

Lenn asks, “What paths are taken by those desperate to escape ruin?”

Is that the impression of a breath, to express a pause which the brevity of this Game does not allow? Thrast asks, “Is this place a threat to my home?”

“Are you going to lead it straight there?”

“Is there a way to stop it?”

“Will you let me point your way to where it cannot follow?”

“Do you intend to interfere where I am going?”

“I will not chase you to the end of your path.”

“Then we are agreed.”

Is it that simple? The breaths complete. Thrast’s transfer proceeds with a shifted destination, sifting him out of Harrows and onto the Bridge where Mere is waiting for him. That Game is bound, now. What more can Lenn do for Veneth and the Circle but to deliver them a yolk and not a maw? He releases his grip on the bonds of the Circle and the immediacy of the Game fades.

Is this how Harrows grows enraged? The jaws that had loomed over first David and then Thrast now encircle Lenn. Is that frenzied buzzing? Are those whispered promises of fates for the pieces of a soul that is shredded by world-fangs? Can this violence even touch him? Could Lenn just ignore it? No. Unfortunately, the threat does #underline[have teeth];.

Lenn asks of Harrows, “Was I ever really here?” He takes no leverage from the Circle, for this is no exchange among peers. Thrast is a #underline[person];. Harrows is just a #underline[realm];. And what did Lenn’s husband take from him? He will defeat Harrows with five questions.

Like Harrows’ threats, can Harrows merely ignore a question posed by the Realmshorn? Does it recognize that it lost the moment it dared reflect back to Lenn the door he most longs to open again? Can it feel the vice closing on it?

Will it answer no, and let the Realmshorn drift free having only #underline[truly] touched the other visitor to the realm? Will it answer yes, and catch itself in the trap? No, it will conjure up a question of its own, and try to play a Game on true terms.

The Wolf and the Wasp ask, “Who is this mocking thief?”

“Do you truly not know?” Four questions remain.

“Are you a wanderer like her?”

“Can you risk a similar meal?” The best outcome is broken teeth.

“Does she not still lead new meals to us?”

“Have you tasted either meal?” A reminder that Lenn has cheated it out of both Thrast and Veneth will goad the realm away from questions about David or his realm.

“Are our jaws at your throat?”

The circle of questions closes. “Am I bound here?”

“And if you are bound?”

Here is the mistake, where Harrows stumbles in the shadow of what it does not understand. Yes, a Game carries risk even for Lenn. But the deed that plucked each thread of connection out of Lenn’s soul and set him adrift in realms forgotten did not diminish his will, but purified his Alignment with Domination. To restore the threads of his connection with any realm, and bind a Dominant Soul to any such place, would transform the nature of that realm itself.

Lenn Declares the truth, “If I am bound here, then this realm is #underline[mine];.”

Harrows concedes, and Lenn vanishes.

= Part Four: Edification <part-four-edification>
= 4.1 Filigree and Refuse <filigree-and-refuse>
== David <david-11>
The phone doesn’t ring. The world doesn’t warp itself around him. It isn’t even like walking through a door. David is #underline[instantly] back on Earth. But it’s wrong.

He is standing in the middle of a bunch of trees, but from bark to leaves they are entirely ash-gray, and stiff. A brief gust blows through in the moments after he returns to Earth, and one of the trees just sprinkles away with the breeze. He takes a couple short breaths, and tries to listen or feel for the looming sense of an open mouthful of teeth, or a hideous buzzing.

The tree graveyard is silent.

David lowers his phone. Ahead, on the ground, there is a man lying on his face. He looks familiar. David steps forward, kneels, and struggles to turn him over.

While he strains, Lila’s voice says over his phone’s speakers, “David.”

“See I knew you weren’t dead,” he says, and grunts, and finally turns over the guy on the ground. It’s MARK. He isn’t breathing.

Lila says, “David, I’m here.”

“Come help me with MARK!” David puts his fingers on MARK’s neck. “No, buddy. Not today.”

She says, “I’m here, but I can’t…

“In a minute!” David shouts. “Help me make him not die!”

Lila says, “You wouldn’t answer. You shut me out.”

He glances at his phone. The call says “Phone-Lila.”

“So you are? Dead?”

“I can feel me everywhere,” she says, “but I can’t quite... Maybe if I can find my body?”

“Lila,” David says, his heart pounding. “You remember MARK? The guy you dumped because he didn’t give enough of his money away? He’s here right now, and he’s dying, and he needs our help.”

“Thrast took him,” she says, her voice a shiver. “Took him fully, like he does the thralls. Then, he pulled himself out. #underline[Everything];.”

David wretches, his gut pulled sideways by Thrast tugging on the other end of the knots they share. Then he starts CPR.

Lila says, “There’s not enough of MARK left to save. Not without me.”

With labored breath, “Okay then where the hell are you?”

“I was here, in body and spirit,” Lila says. “I think I did this to the forest. Fighting Thrast took a lot of raw power. I don’t know if I can find me. I don’t have the same vigor as my original mind. There’s too much noise in here. Too much… you.”

There is a hanging silence, punctuated by David puffing out numbers.

Phone-Lila says, “I have to go away for a minute. If I can focus, maybe I can…”

The silence deepens. David takes a break from compressions. He counts in his head. Another tree is blown away. Some of the ash gets in David’s mouth. There is a faint drilling sound growing behind the sound of his blood rushing in his ears.

Still a little out of breath, he says to MARK’s body, “How did you get mixed up in this, man? What did you do to deserve this? Hell, what did any of us do? Like, #underline[why];? Why Earth? Why me? Why couldn’t it have been some other miserable rock they flicked him over to?” He pats MARK’s chest and rocks up onto his feet.

The underbrush of whatever forest this used to be is dust, scattering in patterns drawn by his feet and the wind. There are no footprints or tracks he can see leading into or out of the little clearing where MARK lies still. A plane flies overhead, lower than David expects. He can’t hear it. That doesn’t change his mind that Something is wrong.

He finds her when another ash-statue disintegrates. It is a massive tree, one of those few in Michigan that were left to grow for almost 100 years and must have dominated its section of the forest. It blows away, and behind it is Lila. She is as motionless as the trees, but her body is poised, her shoulders tense and knees bowed, with her burned skeletal hand and its shining ring raised in a fist like a torch. Tangles of yarn descend from her scarf to the dust on the ground. They weave a great web, out to every tree David can see and to…

Four other Lilas, scattered amongst the trees. Each is as still as the grave, each with a candle flame of spirit in a burned right hand. All these hands are raised. Each pair of lips is curled into the same snarl. Every one of them is zipped up in a jacket. He can feel Lila’s magic hanging in the air, can just perceive the wall that has been constructed around what used to be a forest, can smell iron in the air, and a vague sense of presence falling like a blanket over his soul. He slowly takes out his phone.

Phone-Lila says, “I—she is paralyzed. We—she—I—poured everything into crushing him, and he just blinked away, spared by your arrival. She’s angry. She’s horrified. She’s… that’s all she is David. Feelings. Ideas. Rejection and vitality and sheer directive. With him gone, she doesn’t know what to do. She can’t even think about how to think forward. He broke our mind, David.

But we almost had him.”

He hugs her.

His phone touches her shoulder. A puff of misted breath pours from the mouth of the Lila in front of David. It is followed by a cough, and a sob, and her body buckling in a way that seems almost performative. Her eyes meet his, and she says with her mouth, “I almost had him.”

David steps back, letting her steady herself. He can’t bring himself to speak.

She says, “I’m missing a lot.” And she walks past David towards where MARK is. “I’ve got memories from my time on Veneth with you in place of a lot of the specifics of what happened after my mind got run over by a truck. But I think I’m ‘okay?’” Saying “okay” seems to break her a little bit.

It’s a longer walk than David remembers. He says, “So do you need my phone to be your brain now?”

“No,” she says. “I think I have enough synapses between five bodies to do my thinking for me. Integrating it all might be a little dicey, and without a full Self made from Extension I don’t think I can pilot more than one of me at a time with anything like finesse.”

“I don’t know if I want to talk about that,” David says. “Yet.”

“What, you don’t want to consider the horrifying implications of me turning every living thing in twenty miles of state forest into grist for my Thrast Hammer?”

“Yeah. Can we talk about how you’re going to save MARK instead?”

