People have noticed the forest.

Lila is drifting down towards the highway when she hears the helicopter blades. Somewhere above and behind her, one is approaching the huge swath of protected land she has converted into her other avatars. In the city itself there is a small cacophony, as sirens encircle Thrast’s tower and still more are making their way quickly down country roads to reach the site of her conflict with him. She keeps the barrier in place, permitting only what is hers to pass through it. No need for David and MARK to be arrested and complicate things.

It is hard even to form the invisible boxes she used before Thrast shattered her mind, to Extend her will beyond her body and what it can touch. But it #underline[is] easier to tap into her other four brains and use their processing power to calculate the best ways to manipulate her body’s experience with gravity, friction, and aerodynamics to keep her descent unhurried. The people below will choose to read it how they want, but she hopes a delicate touch will keep them calm…er.

She touches down on the side of an overpass. She remembers, vaguely, that her mind fell around here, but the thing that was recording the details is #underline[the thing she’s looking for];. A cursory glance while trying to ignore the stares of passers-by and the increasing volume of sirens makes her believe it’s not where she sets down, so she jumps down and unfurls a pair of thread-wings. She makes them almost large enough that they should work, but she also asserts with Association that they #underline[do] work#footnote[She can even explain that #underline[their] interaction with the air is closer to that of water, and the force she is able to exert on them because of that interaction allows her to navigate the lighter fluid.];. The metaphysics of this universe, still reeling from the punches it has received in the last 24 hours, do not dare object.

She doesn’t know exactly what to make of her reality being strangely timid in the face of her. Nature has always been an unreadable and indifferent entity, and then only an entity because it serves the human brain to classify it as its own Thing. But now, with Paradigms stepping in on observable facts of existence, Nature has taken on character. Many things, actually, are taking on character. Is this a glimpse of what Thrast felt, both in the place he was from and here, where the whole of reality was clay to him? Is she any less of a menace to Earth than Thrast?

A sheriff calls out to her, the third time she sets down. He has his gun trained on her. “Ma’am, I have to ask you to put your hands up.”

She says, “And my wings?”

“Uh, down?”

“Put the gun down,” she says. “And maybe stop traffic on these roads for… an hour? That will probably be long enough.”

“Ma’am, you are in violation of local and federal airspace regulations.”

“I’ve also violated State Land Use Rule 22 parts e through h, for a start.”

“Ma’am—”

“I’m afraid you’ll need to add several counts of involuntary manslaughter to that list. I lost my mind and went berserk. Put the gun down, sir.” Just in case this one’s trigger happy, she has her jacket shore up its Separation.

“Hands up!” the cop insists.

She sighs and puts her hands up, and folds her wings back into her scarf for good measure. “Don’t try to be a hero. I promise I’m not here to hurt anyone. I’m looking for a phone that got run over on the highway.”

