At some point, you have to take care of yourself first, and all others be damned. So while the police drag their feet encouraging gawkers to clear out, Lila surrounds herself with a dome of Separation and stoops over to touch her broken mind.

She hasn’t had a thought in there since the syncopated trio of crunches that shattered her. Instead she has aimlessly reached for anything that contains meaning or purpose, and found the absence of those things. Somewhere in that memory is a lingering paired meaning: the Mind was not chosen to be saved, therefore the Mind was lost.

Lila digs a burned hand in the debris of her phone. Her ring brushes against shards of plastic, snapped silicone chips, and slivers of glass, and it renders it all into a molten slurry that rolls like mercury into her open palm, and incorporates into her being. Her charred hand gains glistening black nails of plastic and micro-architecture, and its ugly, shriveled flesh is rendered to an almost polished sheen.

Her memory is not restored, but it wasn’t truly gone in the first place. What rushes back into her gestalt being is a certain acuity of intention: where the line exists after she has decided to draw it but before she draws it; the line between the choice and the drawing. Extension fills each Lila, and at once draws them together and distinguishes them.

David is saying, “—just waiting for her to come back and hoping #underline[he] doesn’t come back first.”

Lila turns to David and says, “I’m reconvening. See you in a few.”

She turns to regard the bullets hanging in her wall of Separation like little buzzing insects. She reaches out and closes her ringed hand, bringing the bullets together in a swirl, then turns her hand over and makes a dropping motion. The bullets clink to the asphalt. She lowers the wall and says, “I’ll answer for what I’ve done, but I have some affairs to settle. Find me in what’s left of the forest. Don’t bring weapons, if you have the willpower.” Before they can say anything else, she springs into the air, unfurling another pair of wings and pushing her way toward the forest.

While she is dealing with the officers, David says, “Did you find it?”

She answers, “Enough of it to do what we’re hoping, I think. But I have to all be in the same place.”

