“I’m hoping that it’s window shopping, so I’m keeping the store closed.”

“And you’re okay? With all of this?”

“Little brother,” Lila says, “you have hated what I did with myself every second since you found out it happened. But you have tried, #underline[really] tried, to let it be the choice that I made. I’m glad that you’ve tried, and because you’ve tried, so am I. I am letting you choose to do this to yourself—”

“Enabling me, really.”

“—because I can’t let myself trample people in the name of saving them. And it’s #underline[hard];, David. It’s a puzzle I was trying to tease out, and apparently my heart wasn’t in it, because when my brain got ripped out of the equation I literally couldn’t think about keeping others safe while I crushed him.

“I have to live with that.” She turns, ever so slightly, towards MARK. “People died. I killed them. I won’t let ‘I lost my mind’ be an excuse.”

“I wish I could do something more than this,” he whispers. “I wish all of this #underline[clicked] for me the way it has with you. I wish we could make something out of this mess together.”

“#underline[You] told #underline[me] ‘Don’t sell yourself short.’”

He hugs her.

“Okay,” he says, and he turns away.

== Thrast <thrast-2>
Mere is clever, and she is supported by others from afar. Every time he approaches the door to Veneth, she pushes it out of his reach. Other doors are appearing, confusing the Bridge with worlds he has neither seen nor scarcely imagined. Are these the Paths of which she is Queen? Let her wander these, and leave Veneth to those who belong there. She may push the door out of reach, but if Thrast can find the perfect place to return she will not be able to stop him.

The holy hill of his birth?

Spear clashes with sword in strokes that carry meaning in their violence. Thrast is the Soul of Veneth, the warden of its history, and the shield of its future. It is his responsibility and privilege to safeguard the people of Veneth from the threat of ruin. Mere is the Queen of nothing, with no people under her wing and no investment in the fate of Veneth. She merely carries distaste for Thrast’s methods of governance.

But Mere’s steps are sure. She, too, has seen omens of ruin and the fruit they bear. She has walked many paths too terrible to speak of, and she does it all for the sake of people. Without people, there are no messages to carry. What she will not do is serve as arbiter for meaning. She carries out the wills of worlds, and the will of the people of Veneth is to be free of Thrast’s yolk.

The shores of Goletha, from which he sailed often in his youth?

Mere’s doors cease to obey convention. They appear with no stonework leading down onto the Bridge, hanging in the emptiness beyond. One sprouts up in the middle of the Bridge, turning a thrust of Thrast’s spear well clear of Mere. All manner of doors, and one of them opens.

The door that is opened seethes with whispering until Thrast slams it shut. Once he shuts it, it vanishes, and behind is Mere leading with her sword. Does Thrast fear ruin? Is it the ruin of Veneth, or ruin itself? Because so many realms Mere has walked (and she has walked the most) are cracked open, the order of their first purposes twisted or intermingled or weathered away by time. Now that he has found the secret to step clear of Veneth, can he stop at trying to prevent Veneth’s eventual demise or will he curl his fingers around every realm, digging in each set of guts to stitch together whatever it is he thinks might persevere without falling, one day, into ruin? Things break. Everything is wearing down, in every realm, towards the day when the last stubborn branch of reality finally blows away on the wind.

Qastra, where he took up the mantle of righteous conqueror and carried his purpose out into the world?

Thrast keeps Mere back with the length of his spear. Lies. Mockery. Veneth is a blessed place, deserving of better than the ruin Thrast saw for it. Earth is spectacularly helpless in the face of the souls that inhabit it, and must be guided away from ignorant self-destruction. Even this Bridge, mostly void, has a merit worth preserving against the follies of those who would misuse its gifts. If all things are cursed to the death of meaning and the severance of purpose, then why is it possible for ones like Mere and Thrast to achieve their lofty influence on reality? These gifts demand action and insist that meaning is not a lost cause.

The tower of Ea’river, where he became one with the Ambered Histories?

Mere gestures along the straight, endless path of the Bridge. “Do you want to find out what awaits us at the end? At the edge of this Bridge you will find a Sword, and the one who wields it has the answers that you seek.”

Hundred-halls, the hive of destruction he turned in on itself.

“I have the answers I sought,” Thrast says. “And I will not be turned from the purpose they gave me.”

A door opens beneath his feet, and he passes through.

= 4.4 Tower <tower>
== David <david-12>
When he turns away, Lila doesn’t stay to watch him go. She steps out into the ash-dirt and gives him privacy while he leaves Earth forever. It feels wrong. It aches. And that feels right.

There is what looks like a hole at the bottom of the pool Lila made. The hole is not small. In fact, the longer he stares at it the more he thinks what he’s looking at is not a shallow pool but a well. He can’t see the bottom, and the sides are growing cold. He lowers his foot into the water. All the hairs on his arms bristle, and his irises tighten. His pupils widen, not to collect more light, but because the hook in his gut is bound by an iron cord to the anchor that lies at the bottom of the well. He pulls back against it, managing not to slip down the stairs but take them one heavy step at a time.

He can see the Bridge there, in the dark of Lila’s gate. As the water passes over his head, he feels what he would have expected the switches to feel like. Not faster than a blink, or crossing through a door, but a stretching that carries him both down and up at once. Is this what it is to fall into a black hole? There is no pain, or even pressure, just the sense that his well-defined reality of shape and weight is, in fact, a lump of pure copper being heated and hammered out into a wire like spider silk. Then he has passed through the gate, and stands on a familiar causeway.

It isn’t as still as the grave. A grave implies there has ever been anything but stillness. It isn’t silent. Silence implies there is a way to make sound. Worst, it isn’t indifferent. It has a stated purpose, built into the structure of its being, to transport someone from one location to another. A destination. David cannot take a deep breath, or sigh, or murmur to himself. He can only observe, and then depart.

When David looks behind him, he doesn’t see a door. Instead, it is a shimmering line of light, snaking its way out into the nothing apart from the Bridge. He watches that light slowly fade, as his senses re-stratify, until the path is just a shade lighter than the black it lies against. Apart from the glimmer of Lila’s gate, there are no stairways. Nowhere proposes itself as an exit from the Bridge. This time, there is no one to guide his way.

He starts for the end of the Bridge.

