== Anshall <anshall-1>
Build wisely. But sometimes to build up you must first break down what came before. Even then, one can apply wisdom in many ways, by preserving materials to be reused or discovering a new purpose for something that has lost the ability to serve the purpose it was made for. Anshall returns to the hall where his throne waits, and he crosses the room in quick strides. Aides, messengers, and counselors strain for a clear look at what he carries.

Court no risks. The source of his cowardice. It may have been considered wisdom, to forestall action until the path forward is utterly clear, but it paralyzed him for much of his life. Waiting itself was a risk, one he is paying for now and as dearly as one can pay. He considers his throne, and then chooses not to sit. Instead, he stands in front of it, still framed by its height and weight for the moment when he speaks.

Honor the lives that maintain the sovereign. His father’s father, on learning about what Thrast did to Goletha, should have done this. Then Anshall’s father, on hearing about the razing of their violent neighbor Hundred-Halls, should have acted instead of sighing with relief. And Anshall himself, on hearing first how Thrast became Lord Commander of Qastra’s Shining Legion and then that Ea’river had been forced to pledge itself to him, should have first safeguarded the souls of his people before reaching out to Mere.

He begins, “To the subjects of the Far Halls of Kolomaer and to any ear on Veneth that may hear: a proclamation.”

