= 4.5 The Sword that Seals All Fates <the-sword-that-seals-all-fates>
== David <david-15>
If the Bridge were an actual physical object in a place where physicality represented essential meaning informed by Association, directed by Domination, and sustained by Edification, its relationship to the Sword would need to be communicated with details we can grasp, like the apparent relevant ages of the stone and steel and a webbing of cracks that communicate the effects of the passage of time on both. But none of that is the case, because this realm is old and the Sword is older still, and its apparent position in the Bridge is only a signpost of the effect the Sword has had on the bridge. The Sword is not in the Bridge. It is the angel who is here, almost visible, a messenger and arbiter of the Truth that governs this crossroads. Those on the Bridge who draw near to the Sword draw near, instead, to the threshold of a break in the nexus of metadimensional reality.

David doesn’t realize any of this. He’s standing in front of a giant burning sword buried in the stone of a bridge.

“#underline[Who approaches me];?” Why would someone with so imperious a voice, with such power of command, lay down power and submit to the vulnerability of asking a question?

He answers, “#underline[It’s David];.”

“#underline[Why has David Huxley Adamson come to stand before my vigil?];”

“#underline[I’m supposed to go through.];”

“‘#underline[Supposed to?’];” There’s no air here to shift around them, but David feels a turning. The angel doesn’t need the clarity it asks for. Is that what this is going to be?

David says, “#underline[I’m meant to? Destined to? I have to make sure that Thrast can’t hurt anyone ever again, and to do that I have to go too.];”

The angel asks, “#underline[Do you know what lays beyond?];”

“#underline[Nobody knows];.”

The angel asks, “#underline[David Huxley Adamson, do you fear death?];”

“#underline[Not mine];.”

“Why #underline[do you fear death];?”

“#underline[It’s the end];.” David pauses. “#underline[Isn’t it?];”

“#underline[What is an end?];”

David makes a fidgety gesture to the giant sword that’s on fire and emanating waves of biblical finality. “This #underline[is. An end. The end?];”

The angel asks, “#underline[Does the world end when a person dies?];”

“#underline[For him it does];.”

The angel asks, “#underline[Does it stop turning?];”

“#underline[No,];” David admits.

The angel asks, “#underline[Does a story end when it contents have been told?];”

“It continues in the person who received it, and when that person shares the story with another.”

There is a brief pause of approval for that answer. Then, the angel asks, “#underline[What lays beyond my vigil?];”

David guesses, “#underline[Death? A prison?];”

The angel repeats, “#underline[What lays beyond my vigil?];”

“I#underline[s asking questions the only thing you do?];”

“#underline[How better to administer a test?];”

David’s shoulders tighten. “A test.”

Here, at the end of the Bridge, all is silent. Even this interrogation by a border guard passes back and forth in soundless speech. But David can still feel the other end of the knot in his center, somewhere else, and by the thrumming of it he knows that there is not silence or stillness on the other end.

The angel asks, “#underline[Are you ready to step beyond?];”

David answers, “#underline[No, but I have to.];”

The angel asks, “#underline[What will you do afterward?];”

David asks, “#underline[Is there an afterward];?”

The angel insists, “#underline[What will you do?];”

No. Time to push back. “#underline[What will you do, after I go through? Stay here, holding a line no one crosses, for the rest of eternity?];”

The angel asks, “#underline[What is an end];?”

Angel on the Bridge, do you fear death?

David says, “#underline[A mark in a record];.”

“#underline[And what may come, after an ending?];”

David says, “#underline[A beginning];.” Then, before the angel can ask another question, he asks, “#underline[Do they have what they need, back there];?”

“#underline[Is there assurance I can give that you would accept?];”

He doesn’t answer.

The angel finally stops asking questions. Of course, then, what follows are instructions. “#underline[Set your gaze on the path ahead. Take stock of what you have. Make your path well, and you will be surprised at who may follow.];”

“#underline[Okay.];” David stares at the flames until he has to close his eyes. He lets the knot settle like an anchor deep in his being. “#underline[Okay];,” he says. He shakes his hands out. “#underline[Ready?];”

\~

The Sword stands in one place. It is held by a single pair of hands over every head at the threshold ending every path. There is no blade sharper. It parts realms like smoke.

The Sword is not in the Bridge. It is not on Veneth. It cannot be swallowed by Harrows. It is not hidden in the tombs of Earth. The Sword and the one who bears it set the boundaries and terms of every realm.

What is beyond the boundaries set by the Sword? Few who glimpse beyond are privileged to understand, let alone share those truths.

Now the final steps. Now the Circle closes.

The Sword that Seals All Fates hangs over Thrast’s head.

== Thrast <thrast-3>
A body is unnecessary. Thrast returns to Veneth unhindered by a form apart from the amber star, and this time blessedly not caught under the oppressive weight of the Sworn Sword. He has the time to stretch out, and reconnect with the roots of meaning that have bound him as the Soul of Veneth. His physical presence may be the amber star, lying in the ashes of a long-burnt cathedral, but in moments he is taking in the salt spray from the sea of Goletha and contemplating a churning unrest in Nailport. Veneth seems to sigh with relief at the return of her shepherd.

Even as he flexes his reach, Thrast leverages the Associations within the ruins of Hundred-Halls to ensure that neither Mere nor anyone aside from himself can pass freely into these lands. Separation is a Paradigm of destruction, after all, and Hundred-Halls has already been cut off from the rest of the peoples of Veneth.

There are gaps in his awareness. Several pockets of the sea of Goletha are hidden from him, where Gentlefishers’ lanterns light a path toward peace. They do not remember, but they are doing work he set them on, leaving behind their fortresses and fostering only good in the nations they sail between. Somewhere Thrast cannot see or even pinpoint, Persaval is hunting him. He knows that, unaided, the Sworn Sword cannot travel quickly, but he knows also that Persaval has allies capable of precisely that. Then there is Kolomaer, with which he has no strong Associations and therefore cannot peer into. That nation has managed to slip beneath his notice this long by abiding by his treaties and doing more to facilitate that to hinder, but they will escape his attention no longer. Thrast will begin the work of redirecting the path of his home away from ruin there, in the court of the Sovereign.

It is not far from the ash-flats of Hundred-Halls to the mountain where sits the palatial majesty of the Far Halls of Kolomaer. Not far, especially, when carried on the wind of your own soul. The amber star of Thrast rises out of those ashes and ascends toward the mountain.

Why Kolomaer? Why Anshall, Sovereign of the Far Halls? It is simple calculation. Whatever gifted bond strengthens the Wayward Queen must follow a pattern. There must be five, and they must be founded. There is only one source of Edification so profound as to justify the conspirators’ feats: the Sovereign seat of Kolomaer.

Mere is waiting for Thrast at the border of the kingdoms, and she has Persaval with her. Together, they try to reinforce the Separation that keeps them out to box him in. But the Ambered Histories of Ea’river can be found wherever the prophet of their interpretation can be found, and Thrast has already Extended himself out to seats of his power elsewhere. The amber star appears at the throat of the Lord Protector of Nailport, lost somewhere in the barrens of Qastra, and the body of Thrast’s failed servant becomes a vessel for the prophet himself.

A body is unnecessary, but serves its purposes. This one will fulfill several, the most appealing the poetry in ending the treachery of the Sovereign of Kolomaer by using one of the Far Halls’ own Eternal Blades.

His time on Earth was not in vain. Unbridled creature of destruction that she may have become, Lila Adamson had some strong ideas about the progress of a body through space. He appropriates these tricks for himself, and cuts Bendak’s journey across the Sea of Goletha from a matter of weeks to that of an hour.

In his flight across the sea he passes over a crew of Gentlefishers, and there is a hitch, a quiver of a thought that comes from the strange tether that binds him to Lila’s brother. He pushes it down. The boy was only ever collateral for the conspirators’ spell. In time he will break this connection, or else he will use it to correct the course on Earth as well as Veneth. Until then, he must be expeditious, lest he be exchanged for the boy at the least opportune moment once again.

Mere and Persaval make other attempts to stall him. She opens doors to or from other realms, trying to trick him through or knock him out of the sky, but he is prepared each time, swerving or deflecting each effort. Eventually, it seems, they resolve to stop him in the place of his destination. Indeed, as Thrast descends toward the palace of the Sovereign Persaval awaits atop the great hall itself. His sword is alight with Associative fire, and yet there is a shadow over his face.

There is a barrier of Separation around the whole of the palace, held in place by Persaval’s will. The Sworn Sword says, “You cannot touch him now.”

“The Queen spoke the Sovereign will of Anshall of Kolomaer to enact your failed banishing.” Thrast perches Bendak’s body on the roof. “That act of will is a sin upon Veneth herself.” All the skill of Bendak’s training remains at Thrast’s disposal, so he permits the body to display Thrast’s readiness for a direct meeting of the swords, if that is how Persaval wishes to settle this. “Need I remind you again, Sworn Sword of Ea’river, that you are not a shield? You cannot prevent this judgment.”

Persaval’s flames lick their way down onto the roof of the palace, but they have nothing to feed on but their master’s hatred. “This puppet means nothing to me. Do not expect me to withhold judgment of my own?”

“You will lose control,” Thrast warns him. “You always do. But it does not have to go this way. Relent, and I will allow you to be #underline[my] sword once more.”

“I swore to Ea’river. I swore to Veneth. I swore to Lenn. I #underline[never] swore to you.”

Thrast tries to emit disappointment through Bendak’s form, but the once-protector of Nailport is hardly capable of such an expression. Instead, Thrast carries his vessel forward. “Then I shall shear off the hand that holds the sword, and your last free thought will be despair.”

The very air catches fire. Persaval drives through the distance with his sword leading, forcing Bendak to turn it aside. Sometimes, crossing swords with the Sworn Sword of Ea’river means that your own weapon crumbles in your hands. But Bendak wields an Eternal Blade, from an era when Kolomaer was less careful flaunting the gifts it received from Veneth. Bendak shifts his parry into a counterstroke, slowing Persaval’s charge to a press and a raw offensive into an exchange.

During their comfortable conversation and the narrow-minded pursuit of Persaval’s vengeance that follows, Thrast is left free to focus on something else. He still dances his vessel for the Sword Sword. There are majestic exchanges, harrowing blows, and all the while Bendak’s body slowly deteriorates despite Thrast’s protections. It is enough. Thrast keeps himself alert, ready to seize Mere once she springs on him, and he traces the threads of the woman hiding in a cave who hopes against hope that he never finds her.

Persaval vies. Thrast entertains, and lets his opponent’s Alignment work for him. Persaval closes himself to everything but this: defending the Far Halls and destroying Thrast’s vessel. Meanwhile Thrast issues commands to the Lords Mayor and Protectors of the cities Ea’River to finish preparations for the Legion to occupy Kolomaer while the nation’s leadership transitions. He commissions gates to be erected in the cities whose construction he will personally oversee. And Thrast muses, how Persaval has been in so similar a position before, and just as spectacularly helpless. Begging, pleading for his husband to have the strength of will to overcome Thrast. But Lenn lost his Game with Thrast, and so became Lost. There is nothing Persaval can hope to accomplish here except to stall the fulfillment of Thrast’s will. It is both amusing and disheartening at once to see a sword turned against the anvil that forged it.

And while Persaval defends the king of Kolomaer, Thrast guides a squad of Legionnaires up the slopes a mountain towards a girl in a cave.

When interference finally comes, it does not come as expected. Mere appears as through a door forged from Persaval’s fire, and Anshall steps through behind her.

Mere says, “Enough.”

Persaval moves between them and Bendak’s body. “What are you doing? Go!”

Anshall says, “Persaval, let me speak with him.”

“No!” Persaval says.

Thrast speaks, neglecting for a moment the form of Bendak, “#underline[I will not waste my time listening to the breath of a treacherous Sovereign];.”

Anshall exerts a strength as yet unseen, and pushes past Persaval. “I do not ask permission.”

Persaval’s shoulder slump. He says, “He is going to kill you, and the rest of the Circle will follow.”

“No,” Anshall says. “It ends with me.”

The body of Bendak takes a cautious step forward.

“You came to claim my Sovereignty,” Anshall tells Thrast, “But you cannot have it.” He raises something between them. An edict, stamped with the indelible seal of the Sovereign of Kolomaer. “You will never have it. I have given the full weight of choice to the people of Kolomaer, and I have given the people of Kolomaer to all Veneth.”

Thrast reads for a silent moment. Bendak’s mouth tightens into a downward stroke. “You have condemned all of Kolomaer to death,” he seethes.

“They are building already,” Anshall assures. “They are carrying this message across mountainsides, and onto ships. You cannot catch them all.”

“This is madness. A plague unleashed in the guise of freedom.”

“But at least it is not #underline[you];.”

Bendak’s nostrils flare, and Thrast plunges an Eternal Blade of Kolomaer through Anshall’s chest all the way to the seal of the Sovereign. In that instant, the amber star hanging from Bendak’s neck vanishes.

