= The Circle Closes <the-circle-closes-1>
== Thrast <thrast-4>
The question to him thus, “Whose ruin did you prevent?”

He thinks, at first, that he is having the vision again. He feels that terrible severance, like a cord snapped in the back of his skull, and at once there is the rubble that drifts around him. To think he might be Seeing now is nightmarish, but the truth sheds its skin before him. It begins, not with his witnessing of the Ruin as he foresaw it, but with the flexing of old familiar fingers and the knitting together of eyebrows he once had. Thrast knows this flesh. It is his first flesh, his own body, returned from ashes and cut off from any thread of Veneth’s graces. Now he knows, for certain, though the terrible dawn continues.

The crumbled and senseless detritus he once Saw passes with time, only to be replaced by more desolation. No stone remains upon another. There is no trace of civilization in this place, no sense that any person has touched it with meaningful intent. Thrast stands whole-bodied on the scattered elements of his failure.

What, then, was the purpose of his vision? Why see what was coming, if no terrible lengths of effort could prevent its coming? Thrast lowers himself down onto his face, trying to block out the sight of it. He has toiled for nothing, risking his personhood and wagering the future of his realm on a doom he could not prevent.

And it is gone! Everything he had carried with him, all the weight of growth and loss and sacrifice through centuries of life reversed in one callous instant, denied him as if he had not gathered each piece glimmering, bloodied, or soiled into the deepest part of himself. The grand wizard! The prophet Thrast, He of Titles and Gifts Unutterable, the Soul of Veneth? Ash in the wind.

